{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_0c59b708fdf74cca997beab8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Working in this repository\n2\t\n3\tNotes for coding agents. Humans should read [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) —\n4\tthis covers the same ground plus the traps that cost real time here.\n5\t\n6\t## What this is\n7\t\n8\tA Go engine plus a large body of security knowledge stored as data.\n9\t\n10\t- `cmd/` — the binaries; `internal/` — the engine (`engine`, `extract`, `parser`,\n11\t  `solvers`, `usg` and the rest). Everything outside `cmd/` is internal on\n12\t  purpose: the CLI is the supported interface, so the Go packages stay free to\n13\t  change.\n14\t- `vyql/` — the knowledge: `ontology/` (concepts), `bindings/` (what each\n15\t  language means), `packs/` (rules), `tests/` (specs), `taxonomy/` (CWE/CAPEC).\n16\t  CI fetches a with-tests bundle into `$RUNNER_TEMP` and passes `-data` (CLI) or\n17\t  `$VYQL_HOME` (`go test`). Locally, when `./vyql` is absent, run\n18\t  `./scripts/fetch-free-definitions.sh --with-tests ./vyql` or\n19\t  `make fetch-definitions`.\n20\t- `docs/` — the design series; `docs/README.md` indexes it\n21\t- `benchmarks/` — the measured record\n22\t\n23\t`pattern → concept → binding → rule`. Most coverage work is a new binding, not\n24\tnew Go. Reach for a Go change only after `vyql match` shows the concept is\n25\tgenuinely unreachable from the data.\n26\t\n27\t## The layering is a boundary, not a filing convention\n28\t\n29\tEach layer answers one question, and only that one:\n30\t\n31\t| Layer | Where | The one question it answers |\n32\t|---|---|---|\n33\t| binding | `bindings/` | what does this code *mean* — this call is an `HttpInput` source, that one a `SqlExecution` sink. Technology-specific, judgement-free. |\n34\t| concept | `ontology/` | what kinds of things exist — the language-neutral vocabulary bindings emit and rules consume. |\n35\t| rule | `packs/` | which combinations of concepts are a finding, at what severity, under which CWE. Judgement, no APIs. |\n36\t| engine | `internal/` | how taint propagates, calls resolve, and patterns match. Semantics, no vocabulary. |\n37\t\n38\tThe first three are paths inside the definitions, which this repository fetches\n39\trather than carries. `scripts/fetch-free-definitions.sh` puts them somewhere and\n40\t`-data` or `$VYQL_HOME` points at it. Only the engine is here.\n41\t\n42\tWhen a finding is missed, the diagnosis (next section) names the layer, and the\n43\tfix goes there:\n44\t\n45\t- nothing labelled → binding\n46\t- the vocabulary cannot express it → concept; reuse an existing one before\n47\t  minting — a concept per case instead of per weakness class is how the\n48\t  ontology rots\n49\t- concepts exist and connect, but nothing judges them → rule\n50\t- a real vulnerability with no taint path (authz, protocol state, logic) →\n51\t  emit an existing `code.*Review` concept\n52\t- taint stops at a language construct the engine mishandles → engine, which\n53\t  means a Go change or an issue; **never** a data workaround that fakes the\n54\t  path\n55\t\n56\tTwo moves erode the boundary, and both look like progress in the moment:\n57\t\n58\t- **A binding that encodes judgement** — firing only when the code \"looks\n59\t  dangerous\". Labelling and judging in one place means no other rule can reuse\n60\t  the label, and the judgement hides where nobody audits it. The binding says\n61\t  what the API is; whether that is a problem is the rule's call.\n62\t- **A rule that names one library's API.** Rules are language-neutral; the\n63\t  moment one matches on a path or method name it silently stops covering every\n64\t  other language, and its judgement cannot transfer. Naming APIs is what\n65\t  bindings are for.\n66\t\n67\tThe test for a new binding: would someone who has never seen your motivating\n68\tcase write the same binding from the library's documentation alone? If not, it\n69\tis a repro-shaped patch, and the next variant of the same weakness walks past\n70\tit. This discipline is why tens of thousands of bindings stay maintainable:\n71\teach is answerable to its API's documentation, not to the incident that\n72\tprompted it.\n73\t\n74\t## Three rules that are not preferences\n75\t\n76\t**`go test -count=1`, always.** The Go test cache keys on source files and does\n77\tnot track `.vyql` data. Without `-count=1`, a cached pass hides a change to the\n78\tknowledge base entirely — your edit looks like a no-op and the suite agrees. A\n79\tbare `go test` in this repository is a bug.\n80\t\n81\t**`CGO_ENABLED=1`.** The parsers are C. `CGO_ENABLED=0` fails outright, which is\n82\tthe good case; do not \"fix\" it by disabling cgo.\n83\t\n84\t**Never claim a detection change without a benchmark number.** Recall and\n85\tprecision trade against each other, and a change that improves one while\n86\tsilently destroying the other looks like progress in a unit test.\n87\t\n88\t```sh\n89\tmake build      # -> bin/\n90\tmake test       # full suite, never cached\n91\tmake lint       # gofmt, go vet, golangci-lint\n92\tmake ci         # exactly what the pipeline runs\n93\t```\n94\t\n95\t## Diagnose before you change anything\n96\t\n97\tFour commands answer nearly every \"why did it do that\", and in this order:\n98\t\n99\t```sh\n100\tvyql match ./repro      # was anything labelled? if not, no rule can fire\n101\tvyql resolve ./repro    # did the call resolve? taint stops at unresolved calls\n102\tvyql trace -from X -to Y ./repro   # where does the path actually stop?\n103\tvyql explain ./repro    # the proof tree, and which `unless` suppressed it\n104\t```\n105\t\n106\tA missed finding is almost always one of: nothing was labelled (missing binding),\n107\tthe call did not resolve, or an `unless` clause was satisfied. Reading rule files\n108\tto guess which is slower than running these.\n109\t\n110\tSee [docs/guides/debugging-findings.md](docs/guides/debugging-findings.md).\n111\t\n112\t## Changing detection\n113\t\n114\tThe definitions are maintained separately and are not open to outside changes, so\n115\ta missed finding that needs a binding, a concept or a rule cannot be fixed from\n116\tthis repository. What can be fixed here is the engine: how taint propagates, how\n117\tcalls resolve, how patterns match.\n118\t\n119\tThe layer diagnosis above still tells you which it is. When it names a binding, a\n120\tconcept or a rule, the fix belongs to the definitions.\n121\t\n122\tA change to the engine moves detection everywhere, so measure it:\n123\t\n124\t```sh\n125\t./benchmarks/fetch-corpora.sh\n126\tVYQL_BENCH=1 BENCH_DIR=/tmp/bench/BenchmarkJava   go test -count=1 -v ./cmd/vyql/ -run TestOWASPBenchmark\n127\tVYQL_BENCH=1 BENCH_DIR=/tmp/bench/BenchmarkPython go test -count=1 -v ./cmd/vyql/ -run TestOWASPBenchmark\n128\t```\n129\t\n130\tBoth are gated in CI and must not regress. Current: **+1.00** and **+0.8959**,\n131\tYouden (`TPR − FPR`) macro-averaged. `benchmarks/RESULTS.md` has the method and\n132\tthe per-category breakdown.\n133\t\n134\t**After a benchmark run, change the numbers and nothing else.** This governs\n135\t`benchmarks/RESULTS.md` and `README.md` alike. Replace the stale figures in\n136\tplace and stop: no sentence explaining the run, no fresh caveat about the\n137\tcorpus, no extra table breaking the score down. These are the pages people check\n138\tbefore trusting a score, and they only do that job while they stay short enough\n139\tto read. Every run brings the temptation to explain itself there, and after a\n140\tfew rounds of that nobody reads any of it. A run that moves nothing needs one\n141\tline saying so. Reasoning belongs in `CHANGELOG.md` and the pull request, and\n142\tgit history holds whatever the page drops. Never write a local directory layout\n143\tinto either page; name the environment variable instead.\n144\t\n145\t## Traps\n146\t\n147\t- **Path matching is dotted-segment aware.** `callee.path ~= \"Random\"` also\n148\t  matches `Random.secure`, but not `SecureRandom`.\n149\t- **A score is meaningless without its corpus.** Scores from the synthetic\n150\t  language ports are not comparable to the public OWASP suites. Conflating them\n151\t  has been the single largest source of confusion here.\n152\t- **Loading zero of something looks like success.** A glob that stops matching\n153\t  reports a clean run over nothing. `vyql definitions -kind all` should report\n154\t  concepts and rules in the hundreds and bindings in the tens of thousands. If a\n155\t  count is near zero the data directory is wrong, and every scan is silently\n156\t  under-reporting rather than failing.\n157\t- **Inline control calls inside a concatenation sometimes fail to neutralise.**\n158\t  If `sink(\"...\" + esc(p))` still fires, try `clean = esc(p); sink(\"...\" + clean)`.\n159\t  If that fixes it, the binding's `covers` is what needs changing.\n160\t- **`vyql/` is both a directory and the binary's name.** Binaries build to\n161\t  `bin/`; they cannot sit at the repository root.\n162\t\n163\t## Four repositories ship this, and only one is here\n164\t\n165\tThree satellite repositories document and package the CLI, and none is exercised\n166\tby this repository's CI. Nothing here tells you when they fall out of step, so\n167\tcheck them whenever you change what they describe:\n168\t\n169\t- **[vyprai/vyql-action](https://github.com/vyprai/vyql-action)** — `action.yml`\n170\t  invokes the CLI. It passes `-fail-on`, `-format`, `-profile` and `-exclude`,\n171\t  so renaming or removing any of those breaks every workflow using the action\n172\t  while everything here stays green. Its README states a minimum VyQL version\n173\t  for the flags it passes; that claim has to stay true.\n174\t- **[vyprai/homebrew-tap](https://github.com/vyprai/homebrew-tap)** —\n175\t  `Formula/vyql.rb` is generated from `packaging/homebrew/vyql.rb.tmpl` here.\n176\t  Edit the template, never the tap directly, or the next release overwrites it.\n177\t- **[vyprai/claude-plugins](https://github.com/vyprai/claude-plugins)** — the\n178\t  agent skill, which is instructions naming this CLI's flags. It carries its own\n179\t  copy of `check-skills.py` and a CI job that installs the current release and\n180\t  validates the skill against it, so a renamed flag turns that repository red\n181\t  rather than this one. That check runs weekly as well as on change, because the\n182\t  CLI moves without anything happening there.\n183\t\n184\tWhat actually drifts, in order of how often it has:\n185\t\n186\t1. **Version examples.** Each README shows a pinned version in copy-paste\n187\t   snippets. A snippet pinning a release with a known defect is worse than one\n188\t   showing `latest`, because it is what people paste.\n189\t2. **Install instructions**, which exist in four places: this README, the\n190\t   action's, the tap's, and the skill's `references/install.md`. The install URL,\n191\t   the container image name and the `go install` path appear in all of them.\n192\t   `go install` is the engine only. The satellites that describe it must also\n193\t   name `vyql update -yes`, `install.sh`, or the free bundle on `dl.vyprsec.ai`.\n194\t3. **Flags and severity vocabulary.** `-fail-on` takes\n195\t   `none info low medium high critical`; the action's input description repeats\n196\t   that list, and a change here does not update it there. The same goes for the\n197\t   exit codes: 0 the command run successfully, 1 vyql could not complete, 2 the invocation\n198\t   cannot mean anything, 3 the check ran and did not pass. The action branches on\n199\t   those values and the tap's test block asserts one of them.\n200\t4. **The agent flow**, `scope → scan → coverage → list → verify → reproduce → fix`,\n201\t   is written out in four places: this README, `site/index.html`, the plugin\n202\t   repository's README, and the skill itself. The skill is the one that has to\n203\t   be right, because it is the only copy anything executes. If the others\n204\t   disagree with it they are wrong, not the skill.\n205\t\n206\tThe skill also carries a version that is **not** the CLI's. Claude Code uses it\n207\tas an update cache key, so a skill edit that does not bump it reaches nobody;\n208\tthat repository's CI fails a pull request which changes the skill without one.\n209\t\n210\tAfter a release, confirm the tap resolves to the new version — `brew install\n211\tvyprai/tap/vyql` then `vyql version` — rather than assuming the release job's\n212\tpull request was merged.\n213\t\n214\t## Before you say it works\n215\t\n216\tRun the command and read the output. In this repository specifically:\n217\t\n218\t- a rename that matches nothing looks exactly like a rename that worked — check\n219\t  the linter, not just the build\n220\t- `make ci` green locally is the bar; CI should then agree\n221\t- if you touched detection, quote the before and after numbers\n222\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"03910cfb-c507-4ba8-87ca-06b7e3ef2a8b","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:35:22.008Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/campaign/work/engine-the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan/AGENTS.md","content":"# Working in this repository\n\nNotes for coding agents. Humans should read [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) —\nthis covers the same ground plus the traps that cost real time here.\n\n## What this is\n\nA Go engine plus a large body of security knowledge stored as data.\n\n- `cmd/` — the binaries; `internal/` — the engine (`engine`, `extract`, `parser`,\n  `solvers`, `usg` and the rest). Everything outside `cmd/` is internal on\n  purpose: the CLI is the supported interface, so the Go packages stay free to\n  change.\n- `vyql/` — the knowledge: `ontology/` (concepts), `bindings/` (what each\n  language means), `packs/` (rules), `tests/` (specs), `taxonomy/` (CWE/CAPEC).\n  CI fetches a with-tests bundle into `$RUNNER_TEMP` and passes `-data` (CLI) or\n  `$VYQL_HOME` (`go test`). Locally, when `./vyql` is absent, run\n  `./scripts/fetch-free-definitions.sh --with-tests ./vyql` or\n  `make fetch-definitions`.\n- `docs/` — the design series; `docs/README.md` indexes it\n- `benchmarks/` — the measured record\n\n`pattern → concept → binding → rule`. Most coverage work is a new binding, not\nnew Go. Reach for a Go change only after `vyql match` shows the concept is\ngenuinely unreachable from the data.\n\n## The layering is a boundary, not a filing convention\n\nEach layer answers one question, and only that one:\n\n| Layer | Where | The one question it answers |\n|---|---|---|\n| binding | `bindings/` | what does this code *mean* — this call is an `HttpInput` source, that one a `SqlExecution` sink. Technology-specific, judgement-free. |\n| concept | `ontology/` | what kinds of things exist — the language-neutral vocabulary bindings emit and rules consume. |\n| rule | `packs/` | which combinations of concepts are a finding, at what severity, under which CWE. Judgement, no APIs. |\n| engine | `internal/` | how taint propagates, calls resolve, and patterns match. Semantics, no vocabulary. |\n\nThe first three are paths inside the definitions, which this repository fetches\nrather than carries. `scripts/fetch-free-definitions.sh` puts them somewhere and\n`-data` or `$VYQL_HOME` points at it. Only the engine is here.\n\nWhen a finding is missed, the diagnosis (next section) names the layer, and the\nfix goes there:\n\n- nothing labelled → binding\n- the vocabulary cannot express it → concept; reuse an existing one before\n  minting — a concept per case instead of per weakness class is how the\n  ontology rots\n- concepts exist and connect, but nothing judges them → rule\n- a real vulnerability with no taint path (authz, protocol state, logic) →\n  emit an existing `code.*Review` concept\n- taint stops at a language construct the engine mishandles → engine, which\n  means a Go change or an issue; **never** a data workaround that fakes the\n  path\n\nTwo moves erode the boundary, and both look like progress in the moment:\n\n- **A binding that encodes judgement** — firing only when the code \"looks\n  dangerous\". Labelling and judging in one place means no other rule can reuse\n  the label, and the judgement hides where nobody audits it. The binding says\n  what the API is; whether that is a problem is the rule's call.\n- **A rule that names one library's API.** Rules are language-neutral; the\n  moment one matches on a path or method name it silently stops covering every\n  other language, and its judgement cannot transfer. Naming APIs is what\n  bindings are for.\n\nThe test for a new binding: would someone who has never seen your motivating\ncase write the same binding from the library's documentation alone? If not, it\nis a repro-shaped patch, and the next variant of the same weakness walks past\nit. This discipline is why tens of thousands of bindings stay maintainable:\neach is answerable to its API's documentation, not to the incident that\nprompted it.\n\n## Three rules that are not preferences\n\n**`go test -count=1`, always.** The Go test cache keys on source files and does\nnot track `.vyql` data. Without `-count=1`, a cached pass hides a change to the\nknowledge base entirely — your edit looks like a no-op and the suite agrees. A\nbare `go test` in this repository is a bug.\n\n**`CGO_ENABLED=1`.** The parsers are C. `CGO_ENABLED=0` fails outright, which is\nthe good case; do not \"fix\" it by disabling cgo.\n\n**Never claim a detection change without a benchmark number.** Recall and\nprecision trade against each other, and a change that improves one while\nsilently destroying the other looks like progress in a unit test.\n\n```sh\nmake build      # -> bin/\nmake test       # full suite, never cached\nmake lint       # gofmt, go vet, golangci-lint\nmake ci         # exactly what the pipeline runs\n```\n\n## Diagnose before you change anything\n\nFour commands answer nearly every \"why did it do that\", and in this order:\n\n```sh\nvyql match ./repro      # was anything labelled? if not, no rule can fire\nvyql resolve ./repro    # did the call resolve? taint stops at unresolved calls\nvyql trace -from X -to Y ./repro   # where does the path actually stop?\nvyql explain ./repro    # the proof tree, and which `unless` suppressed it\n```\n\nA missed finding is almost always one of: nothing was labelled (missing binding),\nthe call did not resolve, or an `unless` clause was satisfied. Reading rule files\nto guess which is slower than running these.\n\nSee [docs/guides/debugging-findings.md](docs/guides/debugging-findings.md).\n\n## Changing detection\n\nThe definitions are maintained separately and are not open to outside changes, so\na missed finding that needs a binding, a concept or a rule cannot be fixed from\nthis repository. What can be fixed here is the engine: how taint propagates, how\ncalls resolve, how patterns match.\n\nThe layer diagnosis above still tells you which it is. When it names a binding, a\nconcept or a rule, the fix belongs to the definitions.\n\nA change to the engine moves detection everywhere, so measure it:\n\n```sh\n./benchmarks/fetch-corpora.sh\nVYQL_BENCH=1 BENCH_DIR=/tmp/bench/BenchmarkJava   go test -count=1 -v ./cmd/vyql/ -run TestOWASPBenchmark\nVYQL_BENCH=1 BENCH_DIR=/tmp/bench/BenchmarkPython go test -count=1 -v ./cmd/vyql/ -run TestOWASPBenchmark\n```\n\nBoth are gated in CI and must not regress. Current: **+1.00** and **+0.8959**,\nYouden (`TPR − FPR`) macro-averaged. `benchmarks/RESULTS.md` has the method and\nthe per-category breakdown.\n\n**After a benchmark run, change the numbers and nothing else.** This governs\n`benchmarks/RESULTS.md` and `README.md` alike. Replace the stale figures in\nplace and stop: no sentence explaining the run, no fresh caveat about the\ncorpus, no extra table breaking the score down. These are the pages people check\nbefore trusting a score, and they only do that job while they stay short enough\nto read. Every run brings the temptation to explain itself there, and after a\nfew rounds of that nobody reads any of it. A run that moves nothing needs one\nline saying so. Reasoning belongs in `CHANGELOG.md` and the pull request, and\ngit history holds whatever the page drops. Never write a local directory layout\ninto either page; name the environment variable instead.\n\n## Traps\n\n- **Path matching is dotted-segment aware.** `callee.path ~= \"Random\"` also\n  matches `Random.secure`, but not `SecureRandom`.\n- **A score is meaningless without its corpus.** Scores from the synthetic\n  language ports are not comparable to the public OWASP suites. Conflating them\n  has been the single largest source of confusion here.\n- **Loading zero of something looks like success.** A glob that stops matching\n  reports a clean run over nothing. `vyql definitions -kind all` should report\n  concepts and rules in the hundreds and bindings in the tens of thousands. If a\n  count is near zero the data directory is wrong, and every scan is silently\n  under-reporting rather than failing.\n- **Inline control calls inside a concatenation sometimes fail to neutralise.**\n  If `sink(\"...\" + esc(p))` still fires, try `clean = esc(p); sink(\"...\" + clean)`.\n  If that fixes it, the binding's `covers` is what needs changing.\n- **`vyql/` is both a directory and the binary's name.** Binaries build to\n  `bin/`; they cannot sit at the repository root.\n\n## Four repositories ship this, and only one is here\n\nThree satellite repositories document and package the CLI, and none is exercised\nby this repository's CI. Nothing here tells you when they fall out of step, so\ncheck them whenever you change what they describe:\n\n- **[vyprai/vyql-action](https://github.com/vyprai/vyql-action)** — `action.yml`\n  invokes the CLI. It passes `-fail-on`, `-format`, `-profile` and `-exclude`,\n  so renaming or removing any of those breaks every workflow using the action\n  while everything here stays green. Its README states a minimum VyQL version\n  for the flags it passes; that claim has to stay true.\n- **[vyprai/homebrew-tap](https://github.com/vyprai/homebrew-tap)** —\n  `Formula/vyql.rb` is generated from `packaging/homebrew/vyql.rb.tmpl` here.\n  Edit the template, never the tap directly, or the next release overwrites it.\n- **[vyprai/claude-plugins](https://github.com/vyprai/claude-plugins)** — the\n  agent skill, which is instructions naming this CLI's flags. It carries its own\n  copy of `check-skills.py` and a CI job that installs the current release and\n  validates the skill against it, so a renamed flag turns that repository red\n  rather than this one. That check runs weekly as well as on change, because the\n  CLI moves without anything happening there.\n\nWhat actually drifts, in order of how often it has:\n\n1. **Version examples.** Each README shows a pinned version in copy-paste\n   snippets. A snippet pinning a release with a known defect is worse than one\n   showing `latest`, because it is what people paste.\n2. **Install instructions**, which exist in four places: this README, the\n   action's, the tap's, and the skill's `references/install.md`. The install URL,\n   the container image name and the `go install` path appear in all of them.\n   `go install` is the engine only. The satellites that describe it must also\n   name `vyql update -yes`, `install.sh`, or the free bundle on `dl.vyprsec.ai`.\n3. **Flags and severity vocabulary.** `-fail-on` takes\n   `none info low medium high critical`; the action's input description repeats\n   that list, and a change here does not update it there. The same goes for the\n   exit codes: 0 the command run successfully, 1 vyql could not complete, 2 the invocation\n   cannot mean anything, 3 the check ran and did not pass. The action branches on\n   those values and the tap's test block asserts one of them.\n4. **The agent flow**, `scope → scan → coverage → list → verify → reproduce → fix`,\n   is written out in four places: this README, `site/index.html`, the plugin\n   repository's README, and the skill itself. The skill is the one that has to\n   be right, because it is the only copy anything executes. If the others\n   disagree with it they are wrong, not the skill.\n\nThe skill also carries a version that is **not** the CLI's. Claude Code uses it\nas an update cache key, so a skill edit that does not bump it reaches nobody;\nthat repository's CI fails a pull request which changes the skill without one.\n\nAfter a release, confirm the tap resolves to the new version — `brew install\nvyprai/tap/vyql` then `vyql version` — rather than assuming the release job's\npull request was merged.\n\n## Before you say it works\n\nRun the command and read the output. In this repository specifically:\n\n- a rename that matches nothing looks exactly like a rename that worked — check\n  the linter, not just the build\n- `make ci` green locally is the bar; CI should then agree\n- if you touched detection, quote the before and after numbers\n","numLines":222,"startLine":1,"totalLines":222}}}
