{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917063522b93ade8464d6498c","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The task: the engine's memory safety stop ends a whole-repository scan before any finding is emitted when Python lowering and graph construction over multi-package code exceeds it. Under 4GB cap and 180s timeout, the repository scan writes an empty report.\n\nSo the problem is memory usage during Python lowering and graph construction. The blocked rank is 2794 which depends on `vyql/bindings/c/razer_report_data_size_unclamped.vyql` — a C binding about `razer_report_data_size_unclamped`. Interesting — that's a C binding, but the behaviour mentions Python lowering over multi-package code. So the target repository probably contains both C and Python code (maybe a large multi-language repo), and the Python part exhausts memory before the C part's findings can be emitted.\n\nThe fix should be to reduce memory consumption of the Python lowering/graph construction so that the whole scan fits within 4GB, OR to make the memory safety stop not abort the whole scan but rather degrade gracefully (e.g., stop processing further Python files but continue with the rest / emit findings found so far).\n\nLet me first look at how the memory safety stop works. Search for relevant terms in internal/.","signature":"940661eb167a4de5a0a0ba94"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"e838718e-6783-49e8-a71d-447f290d98eb","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:35:27.232Z"}
