{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026091706413244bf04efa04945c4","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `eval` of a Const is creating 1.75s worth of nodeInline — this is proportional to the number of nodes created. The lowerer is evaluating a huge number of expressions — meaning it is re-lowering function bodies repeatedly somewhere. \n\nKey question: what mechanism lowers function bodies per call site or per class? In this engine, the \"resolved\" calls (e.g., `python.sinks@resolved` in the finding output) suggests binding applicators match resolved calls. But the volume of lowered nodes is the issue.\n\nLet me count: instrument a debug build to count nodeInline calls and print per-module counts. Actually easier: let me look at the inherited-call-resolution — `inherited_call_resolution_test.go` exists. When a call `self.foo()` is resolved to candidate methods, maybe the lowerer *clones* the callee's body for each candidate receiver type (\"method body specialization per subclass\"). In openrazer, `daemon/openrazer_daemon/hardware/*.py` defines ~200 device classes (all inheriting RazerDevice*), and pylib `openrazer/client/devices/*.py` also defines device classes. scripts has effect/macro stuff. The 3-way explosion: e.g., `openrazer_daemon.hardware.mouse.RazerMambaChroma(...)` etc.\n\nLet me measure precisely how many nodes each scan produces. Quick instrument: build a patched binary with a counter printed at exit... that's intrusive. Alternative: use the existing `-graphjson`? or check whether there's a debug/stats output showing graph node counts. Let me check `vyql` command list.","signature":"12285e9b316b4ca2b3c2571a"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"7aacda7a-dfdd-41b3-8974-47726e4c118e","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:41:40.159Z"}
