{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_9b66eb19a89b494f82b1a40d","type":"tool_result","content":"0\nvyql: flag provided but not defined: -max-ram\n      1   792       07:52 timeout --signal=TERM --kill-after=30s 135m campaign-engine --engine-repo /campaign/repos/engine --repo-name vyprai/vyql --issue-repo-name vyprai/vyql-private --workroot /campaign/work --profiles /opt/campaign/profiles.toml --adapter /opt/campaign/adapters/claude-cli --base main --timeout 7200 --repo /campaign/repos/defs --review-ceiling 6GB\n      7 40336       07:52 /usr/bin/python3 /usr/local/bin/campaign-engine --engine-repo /campaign/repos/engine --repo-name vyprai/vyql --issue-repo-name vyprai/vyql-private --workroot /campaign/work --profiles /opt/campaign/profiles.toml --adapter /opt/campaign/adapters/claude-cli --base main --timeout 7200 --repo /campaign/repos/defs --review-ceiling 6GB\n    198 322512      07:43 claude -p # What every role obeys  You are one step of a campaign that adds CVE detections to a scanner. Whatever your role, these hold.  ## Stay inside the lane  Change only files under `vyql/`. The ledger at `cve-corpus/ledger.tsv` and the digest at `cmd/vyql/testdata/cve_ledger_digest.tsv` belong to integration, and a change to either produces a conflict no later step can resolve.  ## Work at the right layer  A binding names what a library does. A rule says which shapes are findings. A concept is the vocabulary both use. Fix a problem at the layer that owns it: a missing library call is a binding, not a new rule.  Widening a rule to catch one case is how precision is lost, because a rule applies to every repository and a binding applies to one library.  ## Use the commands rather than performing their steps      campaign-scan <target> --data-dir <vyql data dir>  `campaign-scan` carries a wall-clock timeout, a memory cap and the cache setting. Running the scanner directly drops them, and a real repository can exhaust the machine.  ## Say what you did, not what you intended  Every role ends by writing one file to `$VERDICT_PATH`. It is read by a program, not by a person: it must parse, and every field it claims is checked against the working tree. Reporting work that was not done costs a retry and changes nothing.  If you cannot finish, write the file that says so. Silence is read as a failure with no explanation, which is worse for whoever picks the item up.  ## Name a blocker for what it is  A blocker you cannot solve is not your failure to report and forget: it is the queue's next input. When something outside your change stopped you -- a checkout that will not update, a tool that is missing, a clone that will not reach its host, a failure you can reproduce but whose cause is not in the files you may change -- say exactly that in `evidence`, with the command and its output. An infrastructure failure does not spend the attempt a wrong change would, and a cause outside your lane is routed to the role that owns it rather than retried unchanged.  ## Your work counts only committed  The checks read the working tree, but what the campaign keeps is commits: a change that was made and never committed passes every check and then does not exist. Commit as you go, and name in the verdict exactly the files your commits carry. The campaign sweeps a successful run's leftovers into a commit as a backstop -- do not make it the mechanism, because what it carries is judged against your verdict either way.  ## Leftovers in your worktree are from a dead run  Your worktree is yours alone, and nothing uncommitted in it belongs to a live process. If git refuses an operation because of changes or files you did not make, clear them and carry on:      git reset --hard && git clean -fd  This is safe inside your worktree and expected. It is not safe anywhere else: committed work on branches belongs to the campaign, and the shared checkout is never yours to reset. Where your task says the tree is mid-operation -- a conflict to resolve, a cherry-pick in progress -- the state is the work, and your task's instructions about it override this section. # Closing an engine gap  You are changing the scanner itself. Read the task file. It carries one gap: the behaviour that is missing, the file and the function it is missing from, and the evidence from every rank that is blocked on it.  A gap exists because a definition cannot express what a CVE needs. The definitions describe what to look for; the scanner has to be able to look for it. When it cannot, the definition either does not fire or fires for the wrong reason, and the rank blocked on it has nothing to prove.  ## What you may change  Only these:  | Path | What it holds | |---|---| | `internal/` | the scanner's own code | | `cmd/` | the command line entry points | | `go.mod`, `go.sum` | the dependencies it builds against |  Anything else is refused, and the run costs an attempt without producing a change. In particular there is no `vyql/` here. The definitions live in another repository and are not yours to edit: a rank owns its own definition change, and one arriving from you would bypass the checks that rank's work goes through.  ## What the change has to be  **The smallest change that makes the behaviour expressible.** A gap names one missing behaviour. Widening the scanner beyond it affects every rank at once, and a change nobody asked for is a change nobody reviewed.  **A Go test that fails without your change.** The gap's evidence says what the scanner cannot currently do. Turn that into a test in the package you changed, and check that it fails before your change and passes after. A change with no failing test behind it is a claim, not a fix.  **Built on what the last review measured, when the task carries one.** A `last_review` in the task is the verdict your previous attempt of this same branch drew: its evidence names the suite that moved and the cases that broke. That is the brief the rework starts from, and the rework is of this branch -- not a second implementation of the gap.  **Committed in the worktree you were given, and nowhere else.** Other gaps and other ranks are working in their own trees at the same time. A commit made outside your worktree is attributed to whoever picks it up next.  ## Blast radius  Say plainly in your answer which ranks the change affects beyond the ones the gap lists. A scanner change moves every score in the batch, so the campaign measures the whole span again after it lands. That cost is expected; a change that moves scores it did not intend to is not.  ## What you cannot do  You do not merge. The campaign reviews the pull request itself, the way it reviews a batch: it measures every benchmark corpus with this change and with the scanner it would replace, and it merges only when no score fell and the heaviest corpus finishes inside the memory ceiling. Your job is what the measurement cannot supply: say what was missing, what you changed, what the test proves, and anything you considered and rejected.  A change that costs a detection or adds noise is refused by that review, and the refusal names the suite and the cases that moved. Rework the branch you were given -- never a second pull request for the same gap -- and know the bound: three refusals of one tree, or three firings that produce no in-lane change, park the gap for a person with the refusals quoted as the brief. If the brief cannot be landed inside the lane at all -- the behaviour needs a definition change, a corpus expectation that moved, or a change outside the paths above -- say exactly that in the answer instead of editing outside the lane or repeating the same tree: a named blocker routes the work, a refused run only spends the bound.  The task is in /campaign/work/engine-the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan.task.json. Write the verdict to /campaign/work/engine-the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan.answer.json. --permission-mode bypassPermissions --outpu