{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917064422e5f5c778b09f4e14","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's already a partition mechanism (`scanPartitions`) that kicks in when the target is too big. But it didn't kick in here — because partitioning is *decided upfront* by some estimate (partition.go: `extract.Partition`?), based on source size. The openrazer repo is small (90 py files, 21k lines) so the upfront estimate says \"one partition\", but the *actual* graph built explodes because of a pathological quadratic behavior in lowering when the three subtrees combine.\n\nSo the real bug: some cross-module lowering step is quadratic (or worse) in the presence of the full 3-package combination. The fix: find that step and make it not blow up.\n\nLet me find where the partitioning decision is made and what thresholds apply — that tells me the \"expected\" source-size-to-graph ratio. Let me look at partition.go.","signature":"8729649b33da4f899e1113c6"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"d05ff25b-8817-417a-97b7-4426d1b1aeeb","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:44:27.312Z"}
