{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_223daeef0587493ba3bfb3e2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tpackage extract\n2\t\n3\timport (\n4\t\t\"os\"\n5\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n6\t\t\"sort\"\n7\t\t\"strings\"\n8\t\n9\t\t\"github.com/vyprai/vyql/internal/extract/frontend\"\n10\t\t\"github.com/vyprai/vyql/internal/extract/frontend/treesitter\"\n11\t)\n12\t\n13\t// PlanPartitions divides the target's source files into groups small enough that one group's\n14\t// graph fits the budget, so a scan under a ceiling analyses the target as a sequence of graphs\n15\t// instead of one. It is the same partition boundary docs/adr/0001 draws around a repository,\n16\t// applied inside a repository that is itself too large for the budget.\n17\t//\n18\t// A scan builds one resident program graph, and that graph's size tracks the target's total\n19\t// analysable source rather than the budget the scan was given. Past a few megabytes of source\n20\t// the graph alone passes a fixed per-scan ceiling, and the run ends with no report at all —\n21\t// the memory watch stops it before the first rule runs.\n22\t//\n23\t// Weights are GRAPH weights, not byte weights: a file is weighed by its size times its\n24\t// language's GraphWeight — how much graph a byte of that language lowers to, in JavaScript\n25\t// bytes, the corpus the memory constants are calibrated on. A byte of Python lowers to about\n26\t// five JavaScript bytes of graph, so a megabyte of Python is weighed as five: without that,\n27\t// a partition sized for the JavaScript corpus overflows the ceiling on dense languages and\n28\t// the scan dies on its first partition. limit and budget are in the same JavaScript-equivalent\n29\t// bytes, so a JavaScript-only target plans exactly as it did before the weights existed.\n30\t//\n31\t// The cost is stated where it is paid: a flow whose source and sink land in different groups is\n32\t// not reported. Grouping walks the tree in path order so a directory's files stay together and\n33\t// the boundary falls between directories wherever it can, which is where cross-file flow is\n34\t// rarest.\n35\t//\n36\t// It takes two figures rather than one, because where to draw a boundary and whether to draw\n37\t// one at all are different questions. limit is the most source one graph may cover under the\n38\t// ceiling; a target inside it is scanned exactly as it was before — one graph, no boundary,\n39\t// nothing to say about it — and PlanPartitions returns nil. Above it, budget is how much source\n40\t// each group holds, and it is the smaller figure: a group sized at the limit sits on the edge of\n41\t// the node detail buffer, and a group that overruns it costs several times as much to bind.\n42\tfunc PlanPartitions(paths []string, excludes Excludes, limit, budget int64) []map[string]bool {\n43\t\tif budget <= 0 || limit <= 0 {\n44\t\t\treturn nil\n45\t\t}\n46\t\ttype weighted struct {\n47\t\t\tpath   string\n48\t\t\tweight int64\n49\t\t}\n50\t\tvar files []weighted\n51\t\tvar total int64\n52\t\tfor _, p := range paths {\n53\t\t\tinfo, err := os.Stat(p)\n54\t\t\tif err != nil {\n55\t\t\t\treturn nil\n56\t\t\t}\n57\t\t\tvar entries []treesitter.Entry\n58\t\t\tif info.IsDir() {\n59\t\t\t\tentries, _ = treesitter.ListAllFilesCounted(p, pruner(excludes))\n60\t\t\t\tentries, _, _ = keepAnalysable(entries)\n61\t\t\t} else {\n62\t\t\t\tentries = []treesitter.Entry{{Path: p, Ext: strings.ToLower(filepath.Ext(p)), Base: strings.ToLower(filepath.Base(p))}}\n63\t\t\t}\n64\t\t\t// Only what a frontend will actually lower counts against the budget. A file no\n65\t\t\t// frontend claims still has to belong to a group — it is what the coverage report\n66\t\t\t// counts as unanalysed, and counting it in two groups would double it — but it\n67\t\t\t// costs no graph, so it must not shrink the group it lands in.\n68\t\t\t//\n69\t\t\t// A file several frontends claim (a .php is both PHP source and config input)\n70\t\t\t// is weighed by the heaviest claimant: what it lowers to is the densest graph\n71\t\t\t// any of them will build from it.\n72\t\t\tclaimWeight := map[string]int64{}\n73\t\t\tclass := frontend.ClassifyEntries(entries)\n74\t\t\tfor _, lg := range frontend.Languages() {\n75\t\t\t\tw := frontend.GraphWeight(lg.Name)\n76\t\t\t\tfor _, f := range lg.FilesFor(entries, class) {\n77\t\t\t\t\tif w > claimWeight[f] {\n78\t\t\t\t\t\tclaimWeight[f] = w\n79\t\t\t\t\t}\n80\t\t\t\t}\n81\t\t\t}\n82\t\t\tfor _, e := range entries {\n83\t\t\t\tw := int64(0)\n84\t\t\t\tif cw := claimWeight[e.Path]; cw > 0 {\n85\t\t\t\t\tif fi, err := os.Stat(e.Path); err == nil {\n86\t\t\t\t\t\tw = fi.Size() * cw / 100\n87\t\t\t\t\t}\n88\t\t\t\t}\n89\t\t\t\tfiles = append(files, weighted{e.Path, w})\n90\t\t\t\ttotal += w\n91\t\t\t}\n92\t\t}\n93\t\tif total <= limit {\n94\t\t\treturn nil\n95\t\t}\n96\t\tsort.Slice(files, func(i, j int) bool { return files[i].path < files[j].path })\n97\t\tvar groups []map[string]bool\n98\t\tcur := map[string]bool{}\n99\t\tvar curBytes int64\n100\t\tcurClaimed := 0\n101\t\tfor _, f := range files {\n102\t\t\t// A single file over the budget still needs a group of its own: it cannot be split,\n103\t\t\t// and refusing it would drop source the scan was pointed at.\n104\t\t\tif curClaimed > 0 && curBytes+f.weight > budget {\n105\t\t\t\tgroups = append(groups, cur)\n106\t\t\t\tcur, curBytes, curClaimed = map[string]bool{}, 0, 0\n107\t\t\t}\n108\t\t\tcur[f.path] = true\n109\t\t\tcurBytes += f.weight\n110\t\t\tif f.weight > 0 {\n111\t\t\t\tcurClaimed++\n112\t\t\t}\n113\t\t}\n114\t\tswitch {\n115\t\tcase curClaimed > 0:\n116\t\t\tgroups = append(groups, cur)\n117\t\tcase len(groups) > 0:\n118\t\t\t// A trailing run of files no frontend claims is not a partition — a build over it has\n119\t\t\t// nothing to lower and reports the target as unreadable. It still has to be counted\n120\t\t\t// once, so it joins the last real group rather than becoming one.\n121\t\t\tfor p := range cur {\n122\t\t\t\tgroups[len(groups)-1][p] = true\n123\t\t\t}\n124\t\tcase len(cur) > 0:\n125\t\t\tgroups = append(groups, cur)\n126\t\t}\n127\t\tif len(groups) < 2 {\n128\t\t\treturn nil\n129\t\t}\n130\t\t// Const-folding reads `.properties` from the whole tree, and a value defined in one group\n131\t\t// and used in another would otherwise fold in neither. They are configuration, not source,\n132\t\t// so they weigh nothing and every group can hold them all.\n133\t\tvar props []string\n134\t\tfor _, f := range files {\n135\t\t\tif strings.HasSuffix(f.path, \".properties\") {\n136\t\t\t\tprops = append(props, f.path)\n137\t\t\t}\n138\t\t}\n139\t\tfor _, g := range groups {\n140\t\t\tfor _, p := range props {\n141\t\t\t\tg[p] = true\n142\t\t\t}\n143\t\t}\n144\t\treturn groups\n145\t}\n146\t\n147\t// keepAnalysable drops the entries no frontend should read: files over the -max-file-size\n148\t// ceiling and minified bundles. It reports how many of each it dropped, which the coverage\n149\t// report shows so a skipped file is never mistaken for a clean one.\n150\t//\n151\t// It is shared with the walk in All so a partition plan weighs exactly the files that plan's\n152\t// scans will lower.\n153\tfunc keepAnalysable(entries []treesitter.Entry) (kept []treesitter.Entry, oversized, minified int) {\n154\t\tceiling := treesitter.MaxFileBytes()\n155\t\tbundleKinds := frontend.BundleKinds()\n156\t\tkept = entries[:0]\n157\t\tfor _, e := range entries {\n158\t\t\tfi, err := os.Stat(e.Path)\n159\t\t\tif err == nil {\n160\t\t\t\tif ceiling > 0 && fi.Size() > ceiling {\n161\t\t\t\t\toversized++\n162\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n163\t\t\t\t}\n164\t\t\t\t// A minified bundle is build output committed as an asset, not source:\n165\t\t\t\t// parsing it costs far more memory than its size, and a committed\n166\t\t\t\t// frontend holds enough bundles to push a bounded scan past its\n167\t\t\t\t// ceiling before any finding is reported. SCA still reads their\n168\t\t\t\t// banners — it walks the tree itself, past this filter.\n169\t\t\t\tif (bundleKinds[e.Ext] || bundleKinds[e.Base]) && minifiedBundle(e.Path, fi.Size()) {\n170\t\t\t\t\tminified++\n171\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n172\t\t\t\t}\n173\t\t\t}\n174\t\t\tkept = append(kept, e)\n175\t\t}\n176\t\treturn kept, oversized, minified\n177\t}\n178\t\n179\t// MergeStats folds one partition's stats into an accumulator.\n180\t//\n181\t// Per-file counts (parsed, oversized, minified, unmatched) sum: a partition sees only its own\n182\t// files, so each file is counted in exactly one of them. Excluded does not sum: -exclude prunes\n183\t// the walk, which every partition repeats in full, so each reports the same figure and the\n184\t// merged one is that figure, not a multiple of it.\n185\tfunc MergeStats(acc, part Stats) Stats {\n186\t\tif acc.Files == nil {\n187\t\t\tacc.Files = map[string]int{}\n188\t\t}\n189\t\tif acc.Unmatched == nil {\n190\t\t\tacc.Unmatched = map[string]int{}\n191\t\t}\n192\t\tfor lang, n := range part.Files {\n193\t\t\tacc.Files[lang] += n\n194\t\t}\n195\t\tfor kind, n := range part.Unmatched {\n196\t\t\tacc.Unmatched[kind] += n\n197\t\t}\n198\t\tacc.Oversized += part.Oversized\n199\t\tacc.Minified += part.Minified\n200\t\tif part.Excluded > acc.Excluded {\n201\t\t\tacc.Excluded = part.Excluded\n202\t\t}\n203\t\tseen := map[string]bool{}\n204\t\tfor _, lang := range acc.Languages {\n205\t\t\tseen[lang] = true\n206\t\t}\n207\t\tfor _, lang := range part.Languages {\n208\t\t\tif !seen[lang] {\n209\t\t\t\tseen[lang] = true\n210\t\t\t\tacc.Languages = append(acc.Languages, lang)\n211\t\t\t}\n212\t\t}\n213\t\treturn acc\n214\t}\n215\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"35fe29d0-f9a3-4755-8fcd-9507b89ce07c","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:44:27.324Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/campaign/work/engine-the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan/internal/extract/partition.go","content":"package extract\n\nimport (\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"p