{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917064724cf1230f859fd4f69","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"WAIT. With `-max-ram 20GB`, the full 3-way scan completed in 8.8 SECONDS: extract 0.5 + lower 2.0 + sca 0.7 + bindings 5.6 + evaluate 0.02. Total 8.8s! And it produced 53 findings at or above high.\n\nBut under `-max-ram 4GB`, the same scan dies at 3.3GB RSS in ~8s?? The whole scan only takes 8.8s and completes. So the memory usage must peak somewhere and the difference between 4GB-cap runs and 20GB-cap runs...\n\nOH WAIT. I see. `applyMaxRAM` — the flag doesn't just arm the watcher: it *partitions the budget across the pools a scan provisions* (graph store caches, node detail buffer etc.). Under 4GB, the pools are sized small, and maybe the in-memory store falls back to the disk-backed BadgerGraph... and THAT's what explodes! Look at badgergraph.go: \"Badger's block/value cache (sized to the user's --max-ram). When RAM is ample the graph is [in memory]\". So under 4GB the graph store might go to disk-backed badger, and with the cache sizes tiny, badger thrashes — memtables flush, SST builds, block cache churn — eating far more memory and time than the in-memory store.\n\nAnd the heap profile's alloc_space showed: z.Calloc 1.8GB, badger skl.newArena 873MB, table.Builder 347MB, WriteBatch.Set 225MB — YES! Badger was being used in the 4GB run. The in-use at stop showed z.Calloc 724MB (badger arena).\n\nSo the real behavior: under the mandated 4GB cap, `applyMaxRAM` decides the graph cannot fit in RAM (some budget computation decides to use the disk-backed store?), and the disk-backed path allocates enormously and/or the memory stop threshold (3.3GB) is crossed because badger's memory + the lowered graph in Go heap + everything else exceeds it.\n\nWait, but hold on: is that decision based on source size (predicted graph size) vs budget? Let me read applyMaxRAM.","signature":"0778ebf2e3fe4dd58006cb12"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"9a0a2976-8acf-4964-8d1b-84c82824469f","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:47:36.179Z"}
