{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609170647367bcd6cf792574fb9","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now the mechanism is clear:\n\n**With `-max-ram 4GB`:**\n- `applyMaxRAM` sets `lowering.DiskStorePath` → the graph goes through the **disk-backed BadgerGraph store**.\n- `detailBufferBytes(4GB)` = clamp(4GB/8, min(64MB, 512MB), 2GB) = clamp(512MB, 64MB, 2GB) = **512MB** detail buffer.\n- `oneGraphSourceBytes(4GB)` = 512MB / 64 = **8MB** of JS-equivalent source for one graph. Python weighs ~5× (GraphWeight), so 8MB/5 ≈ **1.6MB of Python** before partitioning. openrazer's Python is ~700KB → under the limit → ONE graph, no partitions.\n- The scan lowers into the BadgerGraph (disk store) with 512MB detail buffer and 256MB... wait DiskCacheBytes = clamp(4GB/16, min(64MB, 256MB), 512MB) = clamp(256MB, 64MB, 512MB) = 256MB block cache.\n\nThen WHY does it blow to 3.3GB in 8 seconds when the same scan in-RAM (20GB ceiling → in-mem store? no wait — 20GB also sets DiskStorePath!)...\n\nHmm wait, with -max-ram 20GB: DiskDetailBuf = clamp(2.5GB, 64MB, 2GB) = 2GB; DiskCacheBytes = clamp(1.25GB, 64MB, 512MB) = 512MB. And the scan completed in 8.8s. So with a 20GB ceiling it still used the disk-backed store (DiskStorePath set), with a 2GB detail buffer.\n\nThe 4GB run: 512MB detail buffer. The whole scan's node detail for 1.29M nodes × ~200 bytes ≈ 260MB... hmm, that fits in 512MB. But the earlier alloc profile showed detWriter.str allocating 1.6GB and badger writes happening — so detail DID spill (or double-buffer).\n\nThe one-graph source limit: 8MB JS-equivalent. Let me compute openrazer's Python weight: Python GraphWeight — let me check. If Python weight is 500 (i.e., w/100 = 5), then 700KB × 5 = 3.5MB JS-equivalent < 8MB → one graph. OK.\n\nSo under 4GB: one graph, disk-backed store, 512MB detail buffer, 256MB badger cache, Go heap limit = goHeapMemoryLimit(4GB) (probably 4GB minus reserve ~ 3GB?), and the resident watchdog stops at 3.3GB.\n\nThe run dies at 3.3GB. In-use Go heap at death: 1.6GB (from the profile). RSS 3.3GB = Go heap (1.6-2GB) + badger mmap (z.Calloc 724MB in-use — ristretto's z allocator uses Calloc for memtable arenas etc.) + C-side tree-sitter... So badger's memory-mapped stuff is ~700MB-1GB.\n\nBut WAIT — the 20GB run finished in 8.8s, so the disk store isn't inherently slow. The 4GB run reached 3.3GB RSS in ≤8s. What's different?\n1. Detail buffer 512MB vs 2GB — with 512MB, detail spills to badger; spilling means SST flushes; reading back during bindings means block cache churn (256MB). The pair scan (pylib+daemon, 1.14M nodes) took 18.4s under 4GB and *succeeded*. Hmm! So spilling isn't fatal per se.\n2. Go heap memory limit: `debug.SetMemoryLimit(goHeapMemoryLimit(4GB))`. For 4GB that's maybe ~2.8-3GB. When the Go heap nears the limit, GC runs continuously (death spiral: GC assist). That's the CPU side; but the resident sample includes badger mmap, so Go heap limit + badger mmap together can cross 3.3GB while each is individually \"in budget\".\n\nActually let me test: run the 3-way combo under 4GB but WITHOUT the memwatch... can't disable. Alternatively run with `-max-ram 8GB` and `-max-ram 6GB`, `-max-ram 5GB` to find the threshold.","signature":"3dbe3c6727b242a7a24e7320"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"4fe37fe3-0dad-4515-8100-b639089a3ed6","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:47:52.811Z"}
