{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917064911dfa9d836c96b46fd","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"- 6GB: completes in 32s, 53 findings.\n- 8GB: completes in 42s (slower?? more cache → different behavior... whatever).\n- 4GB: dies at 3.3GB in ~8s.\n\nSo the 4GB failure is specific. Note 6GB works but takes 32s — within the 180s timeout, fine. 4GB is the mandated cap.\n\nNow, why does 4GB blow up so fast (8s)? The Go heap limit under 4GB is `goHeapMemoryLimit(4GB)`. Let me look at that function and at how the scan under 4GB behaves — specifically whether the lowering into the disk store with a 512MB buffer explodes on this corpus.\n\nWait, actually — hold on. Let me re-examine: the pair scan pylib+daemon under 4GB took 18.4s and succeeded. The 3-way combo under 4GB dies at 3.3GB within 8s. But under 20GB, the 3-way takes 8.8s total. Something about the 4GB configuration makes the *same* work take unbounded memory.\n\nKey differences under 4GB:\n1. DiskDetailBuf = 512MB (vs 2GB at 20GB cap). 1.29M nodes' detail might exceed 512MB → spill → badger flush → but also **read-back during bindings**.\n2. DiskCacheBytes = 256MB.\n3. goHeapMemoryLimit(4GB) — probably ~2.5-3GB soft heap limit.\n\nThe death at 8s: the phases at 20GB were extract 0.5s, lower 2.0s, sca 0.7s, bindings 5.6s. Death at 8s ≈ during bindings (same timeline!). So lowering completed even under 4GB; the memory was consumed during **bindings** phase, where the detail spilled to disk gets read back.\n\nAnd what does the binding matcher do with node detail? `bindings.ReleaseStoreIndexes` hints at per-store indexes. The binding phase probably builds indexes over all nodes (match indexes), and each index holds node detail in Go heap. With the disk store, detail reads materialize through the block cache... but 1.6GB in-use at death was mostly lowering allocs.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually — let me reconsider. In-use at stop: 1.6GB total, of which lowering-created graph stuff ~1GB (intern 213MB + AddNode 332MB + propsWithInline 211MB + evalCall 39MB + nid 52MB...). And z.Calloc 724MB (badger). RSS = 3.3GB = Go in-use 1.6GB + Go heap slack/freed-but-not-returned + badger mmap 724MB+ + tree-sitter C.\n\nSo the immediate cause of crossing 3.3GB is that the Go heap itself (with slack) plus badger's mmap plus the C parse trees exceed the reserve — even though each individual budget was \"carved out\".\n\nBut then why did the pylib+daemon pair (1.14M nodes, 90% of the graph) survive under 4GB? Its detail also spilled? Maybe just under the line.\n\nLet me be scientific. Instrument the actual runs: run 4GB with GODEBUG=gctrace=1 to watch the heap grow.","signature":"630d69c0ac594f4b82529560"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"31ee0f08-a901-477f-a688-de14480f20e7","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:49:24.603Z"}
