{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026091706493518dc6eaae1954a92","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The Go heap: live ~1.8GB at gc48 and growing; heap goal 2.7GB. So the *live* Go heap is growing steadily past 1.8GB during bindings. Note the memory limit seems not to be capping at ~2.7GB goal... The watchdog stops at RSS 3.3GB.\n\nUnder 20GB (or no cap), the whole scan's peak live heap must be around 2GB and it finishes before RSS crosses the 4GB-derived stop point. The problem: this scan's *live* memory (~2GB+ and climbing when killed) + badger mmap + parse trees > 3.5GB stop threshold. So the graph + binding indexes for this corpus genuinely need ~2.5-3GB RSS under the disk-store configuration.\n\nBut wait — the 6GB run finished in 32s. What's goHeapMemoryLimit(6GB)? Probably 6GB×something. The scan fits under 6GB's thresholds. Under 4GB the same work doesn't fit.\n\nNow: is the *work* itself pathological, or is it \"legitimately\" 2.5GB of live graph for 21k lines of Python? 1.29M nodes for 21.5k lines = 60 nodes/line, and ~2KB of live memory per line of Python. Compare: daemon alone = 876k nodes for 15k lines = 58 nodes/line. So node density is the same; the 3-way graph is additive in nodes. The live heap grows during *bindings* though — gc43@3.7s live 1281MB → gc48@7.5s live 1806MB. Bindings index the graph...\n\nHmm wait, actually, let me look again at the earlier full-run timings: bindings 5.6s under 20GB. Under 4GB, we died at 7.5s wall — extract+lower+sca took ~3.2s (similar), so we were ~4s into bindings when killed, with live heap 1.8GB and growing. Under 20GB the run finished at 8.8s. So under 4GB we were nearly done (maybe a second away) when the watchdog killed us!\n\nThe difference between finishing and dying is that under the 4GB config the resident memory (Go heap 1.8-2GB live + slack + badger 700MB-1GB mmap + C trees) crosses 3.3GB just before the end. Under 6GB, the stop point is 6GB-768MB=5.2GB — enough headroom to finish.\n\nSo the scan is *this close*: it needs ~3.4-3.6GB RSS peak but is stopped at 3.3GB. The fix must reduce peak resident memory of this scan shape (Python multi-package) by ~10-20% — or better, find the real memory waste.\n\nWhere's the waste? Candidates:\n1. **Badger store under -max-ram 4GB**: the graph goes to the disk store even though the whole graph fits in RAM comfortably (the in-mem store would use less total than badger's mmap arenas + block cache + write buffers?). Interesting: with 20GB the same disk store is used (DiskStorePath set whenever -max-ram is given), and it finished. The badger overhead (z.Calloc 724MB) is pure overhead vs the in-mem store.\n\nWait — actually, does the in-mem store get used when NO -max-ram is given? The `vyql graph` command ran without -max-ram → in-mem store → finished in <30s with modest memory. So the natural fix might be: don't route to the disk store when the target is small enough that the graph fits (the source-limit check exists! `oneGraphSourceBytes`), OR reduce badger overhead.\n\nHold on, let me re-read applyMaxRAM: it ALWAYS routes through the disk-backed BadgerGraph (\"routes the graph through the disk-backed BadgerGraph store so node detail can leave RAM. (A target that goes over the one-graph limit is partitioned instead, and each partition is small enough to hold in RAM — see scanPartitionsCollect for why the disk store is bypassed there.)\").\n\nSo: -max-ram → disk store always. Partitions → in-RAM store (UseIntStore=true).\n\nHmm OK. Now what exactly is the marginal memory hog in the 4GB config? Let me measure the components:\n- Go live heap at end: ~2GB (from gc trace: 1806MB and growing at 7.5s; final maybe ~2GB)\n- Badger: z.Calloc in-use 724MB at stop\n- Tree-sitter C parse trees: freed after lowering? \"Even with cross-scan caching disabled, a bounded scan installs a transient body spool\" — bodies are released.\n\nRSS 3.3GB ≈ 2GB Go live + ~0.4GB Go slack + 0.7GB badger + 0.2GB other.\n\nNow compare with what the resident threshold allows: 3.5GB (4GB - 512MB reserve... wait memoryStopThreshold(4GB): reserve = max(4GB/8=512MB, 256MB) capped at min(2GB, quarter=1GB) → 512MB. Stop at 3.5GB. The message said \"reached 3.3 GiB\" — so it crossed 3.5GB(=3.25GiB) — yes 3.5GB = 3.25GiB ≈ \"3.3 GiB\" displayed).\n\nSo the scan's peak RSS is ~3.5GB+, just over. It needs to shed ~300-500MB, or more robustly ~1GB.\n\nOptions:\nA. **Don't use the disk store when the target is under the one-graph limit AND the graph would fit**: the in-mem int store might use less peak memory than badger (no mmap arenas, no block cache, no SST builders — all that badger write path memory: newArena 873MB alloc, table.Builder 347MB, WriteBatch 225MB, logFile.encodeEntry 122MB...). That's a LOT of allocation churn from badger just to hold a graph that fits in RAM.\n\nActually wait — but if the in-mem store holds node detail in Go heap, and detail is ~500MB-1GB for 1.29M nodes... the badger route spilled detail to disk precisely to keep it out of RAM. With 512MB detail buffer + spilling, in-Go-heap detail is capped at 512MB. Hmm.\n\nBut look at the in-use profile again: intern 213MB + AddNode 332MB + detWriter.str... the *structural* graph (nodes/edges in badger via intern?) — BadgerGraph.intern allocates 928MB total, 213MB in-use. What is \"intern\"? Interning node IDs? Let me read badgergraph.go and the lowering's store path to understand what memory goes where. Also `usg.Freeze`.\n\nActually — step back. Let me quantify precisely: what does peak RSS look like for the SAME corpus with the in-mem store (no -max-ram)? The `graph` command finished <30s. Let me run `scan` with NO -max-ram... but then armMemoryWatch uses 80% of machine RAM (25GB) — fine, it'll finish. Let me measure its peak RSS by sampling.","signature":"5c653be42b074ba9b72fa7c3"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"21991488-51c0-472a-8948-52022b24dc3a","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:50:03.070Z"}
