{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917065022ab0fdbfecc944c35","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the store selection. Let me now precisely account for the memory in the 4GB (badger) run vs the in-RAM run. The in-RAM run peaked at 1259 MiB. The badger run was killed at ~3.5GB and still climbing.\n\nComponents in the badger run:\n1. Go heap live ~1.8-2GB at kill (vs in-RAM run's total peak 1.26GB — including everything!). So the Go heap in the badger route is *bigger* than the whole process in the in-RAM route. Why? Because:\n   - The lowering state + binding indexes are the same...\n   - Wait, why would the Go heap be bigger with badger? Because badger holds its write path (memtables, arena, SST builders, ristretto block cache — `z.Calloc` 724MB in-use!) as Go-process memory. z.Calloc is C-allocated (via C malloc or mmap?) — ristretto z.Calloc uses mmap (C memory) — counted in RSS but not Go heap. But `skl.newArena` (873MB alloc / 83MB in-use) is Go heap. flatbuffers.NewBuilder (1.5GB alloc) — churn.\n   \n   Actually the gctrace showed live heap 1.8GB growing at kill. In the in-RAM run, the whole process peaked at 1.26GB. The graph structure itself (int store) fits in well under that. In the badger run, the *graph structure also has to be somewhere* — BadgerGraph keeps the structural graph (adjacency?) in RAM plus detail on disk? Let me read badgergraph.go fully.","signature":"4be896884b234236897f8ff4"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"4193a407-ea3b-4ebb-9b70-b31036b735f3","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:50:30.470Z"}
