{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609170650302e8a2df5a3da4638","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the BadgerGraph design:\n- Structural core stays in RAM: `idx map[string]int32` (1.29M string keys!), `ids []string`, `typs []string`, `out [][]iedge`, `in map[int32][]iedge`, `flowOut/flowIn [][]int32` (packed at Freeze), `labels`, `byType`, `byConcept`, `conceptHas` (nil'd at Freeze).\n- Detail spills to badger past detCapByte (512MB under 4GB).\n\nSo even in the \"disk-backed\" store, the *whole structural graph including the id→idx map* is resident. The in-RAM int store holds the same structure but, presumably, detail too... and yet the in-RAM store run peaked at 1259MB TOTAL. So how did the badger run reach 3.5GB?!\n\nDifferences:\n1. In the in-RAM (IntStore) case: `detailBufferBytes` doesn't exist; detail is in the store's own maps (compactly?).\n2. In the badger case: detail is *additionally* buffered in `g.det` up to 512MB, *then written to badger* — the write path costs badger memtables (32MB arenas ×2), SST builders, block cache 256MB... and each spill RE-ENCODES. But the detail buffer is capped at 512MB, flushed entirely. So g.det is at most ~512MB. Hmm.\n\nWait, but look at flushDet: it flushes when detBytes >= detCapByte. detBytes is *approximate* — `estDetBytes` counts string bytes + 96 + props. It *underestimates* Go overhead (map[int32]nodeDetail slots, struct alignment)... but roughly right.\n\nHmm, but wait — what happens *after* the flush? `g.det = map[int32]nodeDetail{}` — but AddNode keeps adding to it. So steady-state g.det ≈ 512MB. That's 512MB of Go heap ON TOP of what IntStore would hold for detail (which for this graph is maybe 300-500MB too, but then IntStore's total was 1.26GB).\n\nActually wait, there's a subtle poison: does anything hold ALL node details in Go heap in the badger path but not the intstore path? Look at the *binding* phase: `bindings.Apply` → store indexes → `RangeNodeIndexes` — iterates all nodes, building indexes. Each `nodeAt(i)` decodes or reads resident. Fine.\n\nLet me just measure the two runs' phases: in the 4GB run, lower=2s then bindings grows heap from ~1.3GB to 1.8GB+ and gets killed. In the no-cap run the whole scan peaks at 1.26GB. So the badger run's *live heap* is ~1GB larger than the no-cap run's *total RSS*. Where?\n\nCandidates:\n- g.det 512MB detail buffer (Go heap)\n- badger write-path Go heap: memtable arena 32MB×2, WriteBatch buffers, table.Builder (alloc 347MB churn)\n- badger block cache: ristretto, z.Calloc 724MB in-use — ristretto's cache is Go heap? z.Calloc uses C malloc (off Go heap, in RSS).\n- The ristretto z allocator: 724MB in-use at stop. That's huge! BlockCacheSize = 256MB was configured... but z.Calloc 724MB in-use. Where else does z.Calloc come from? Badger's value log? Memtables are Go heap (skl.newArena is Go). Actually badger v4's block cache is ristretto/z.Cache which Callocs 256MB up front? Plus index cache set to 0. Hmm, 724MB ≈ 256MB + something. Or the mmap'd SST files (mmapread) counted in z.Calloc? Badger memory-maps the value log and tables; the resident portion counts toward RSS.\n\nOK — measuring beats speculating. Let me instrument the 4GB run with GODEBUG=gctrace (already did: live heap 1.8GB growing at kill during bindings) and get a heap profile at a *later* moment... we have /tmp/heap.prof from the first 4GB run killed at 8s: in-use 1.64GB. Breakdown: lowering graph structures ~1.1GB (intern 213MB, AddNode 332MB, propsWithInline 211MB, evalCall 39MB, nid 52MB, AddFlowEdgeIfPresent 22MB...), z.Calloc 724MB, badger skl.newArena 83MB.\n\nCompare that against what IntStore would hold for the same graph. The no-cap run peaked at 1259MB total RSS — including Go heap + C trees + everything. So the badger path's Go heap alone (1.64GB) EXCEEDS the intstore path's entire process (1.26GB)!\n\nWhy is the badger path's Go heap bigger? Two things:\n(a) g.det detail buffer: under the 4GB cap, up to 512MB of detail resident as nodeDetail structs (with per-detail overhead underestimated). In IntStore, detail is stored... let me look at intstore.go to compare.\n(b) The `intern` map idx map[string]int32 for 1.29M long string ids — IntStore has the same.\n\nHmm wait — actually, does IntStore store props more compactly? Let me look at intstore.","signature":"fe60b93adf3a400ebb8bf701"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"ddfda162-88d6-4352-b227-655d78b08027","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:50:53.511Z"}
