{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026091706505303d936e1fd114c91","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"IntStore holds detail in *parallel slices* (compact). BadgerGraph holds detail in `map[int32]nodeDetail` (a map with struct values including 9 string headers ≈ 9×16 bytes + map overhead per entry ≈ 150+ bytes/node before content) *up to 512MB estimated bytes*, plus badger's write path + cache.\n\nSo the badger route for a graph that fits in RAM costs:\n1. `g.det` detail buffer: up to detCapByte (512MB under 4GB) of *estimated* bytes — the estimate (96 + strings + props) *undercounts* the real map/struct cost (Go map[int32]nodeDetail entry ≈ 8+sizeof(nodeDetail≈160)+overhead ≈ 200B vs estDetBytes' 96 baseline) — so real resident could be ~1.5-2× the 512MB \"cap\", i.e. ~0.8-1GB.\n2. Badger block cache 256MB (z.Calloc, C memory in RSS).\n3. Badger memtables/write batches/SST builder churn — transient but real (Go heap high-water).\n4. Same structural core as IntStore.\n\nThat plausibly adds ~1-1.5GB over the IntStore path — matching the observed 3.5GB vs 1.26GB.\n\n**The smallest correct fix**: the bounded path should not take the disk-backed store when the target's source is small enough that the graph demonstrably fits in RAM under the ceiling. And indeed the machinery to decide that already exists: `oneGraphSourceBytes(ceiling)` = the most analysable source one resident graph may be built from under the ceiling (8MB JS-equivalent for 4GB). The openrazer Python is ~3.5MB JS-equivalent → under the limit → \"one graph\" — but applyMaxRAM *unconditionally* routes to the disk store for the whole scan, even for a one-graph target.\n\nWait — re-read applyMaxRAM's comment: \"it partitions the budget across the pools a scan provisions, applies the heap ceiling, and routes the graph through the disk-backed BadgerGraph store so node detail can leave RAM. (A target that goes over the one-graph limit is partitioned instead, and each partition is small enough to hold in RAM — see scanPartitionsCollect for why the disk store is bypassed there.)\"\n\nSo the intended design: \n- target > oneGraphSourceBytes → partitioned, each partition scanned in-RAM (UseIntStore=true — set in scanPartitionsCollect which OVERRIDES the disk store!). \n- target ≤ oneGraphSourceBytes → one graph, disk-backed store, detail can spill.\n\nSo for a one-graph target under a ceiling, we ALWAYS pay badger. And as measured here, for a target that comfortably fits in RAM (1.26GB peak with IntStore vs 3.5GB+ with badger), the disk store *costs* memory instead of saving it — the exact opposite of its purpose, and enough to trip the safety stop on a scan the ceiling could easily afford.\n\nNow what is the *smallest change that makes the behaviour expressible*? The gap: \"the engine's memory safety stop ends a whole-repository scan before any finding is emitted when the Python lowering and graph construction over multi-package code exceeds it\". The fix should make scans like openrazer complete under 4GB/180s.\n\nOptions:\nA. In `applyMaxRAM`, only route through the disk store when the target's source actually needs it — i.e., when the target exceeds the one-graph limit... but then what IS the disk store for? The partition path uses the int store. Hmm — the one-graph limit is defined as \"the most analysable source one resident program graph may be built from under the ceiling\": `detailBufferBytes(ceiling)/detailBytesPerSourceByte`. For 4GB that's 8MB JS-equivalent. openrazer is 3.5MB → fits. A 20MB-JS-equivalent target → partitioned (in-RAM per partition). So when is the disk store ever the right choice? When PlanPartitions... wait, PlanPartitions is only invoked when? Let me check where scanSourceLimit is used and how partitions interact with the disk store.\n\nLet me find where PlanPartitions is called in main.go.","signature":"b5c8b934274e48ce93c74e96"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"8bec07cb-e9b2-4161-b9e2-d10567c264c1","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:51:07.921Z"}
