{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917065201889aed52dfb448ca","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The 4GB scan STILL dies at 3.3GiB even with the in-RAM int store! So the badger store is NOT the main culprit. The difference between the no-cap run (1259MB peak) and the 4GB run is then:\n1. `debug.SetMemoryLimit(goHeapMemoryLimit(4GB))` — the soft heap limit.\n2. The watchdog itself (should not change allocation).\n3. The transient body spool (`parsecache.OpenTransient`) — the \"bounded scan installs a transient body spool\" (in the badger path only).\n4. The int store fallback in this run vs... wait, in the no-cap run the store was ALSO int store (DiskStorePath empty). So stores match now. The remaining difference: **the Go soft memory limit**.\n\nHow can a soft memory limit make resident memory BIGGER? `debug.SetMemoryLimit` sets GOMEMLIMIT. When the live heap approaches the limit, the GC runs more frequently but does NOT free live memory — and crucially, with GOGC ignored under a soft limit... hmm. Actually GOMEMLIMIT shouldn't increase RSS; if anything it shrinks it. Unless... wait.\n\nHold on, let me re-check: in this TMPDIR run, did the fallback branch actually trigger? newGraphStoreDir — where does it create the dir? Let me check graphstore.go — it might use os.MkdirTemp under os.TempDir() (respects TMPDIR) or somewhere else. If it failed, stderr would show... the fallback branch prints nothing! It silently sets UseIntStore=true. Hmm, but ALSO: the fallback does NOT install the transient body spool (that's in the success path). Fine.\n\nBut wait — did it die at the same 3.3GiB with the same timing? Let me measure the peak more carefully, and let me also get a heap profile of this run to compare in-use with the badger run. Also check: maybe newGraphStoreDir succeeded despite TMPDIR (e.g., uses /campaign/tmp or XDG). Let me read graphstore.go.","signature":"b62f9160e1754a96830a05b0"}],"stop_re