{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026091706531029ae520958944a17","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"**Peak 1220 MiB, 7 seconds, 53 findings** under the full mandated 4GB ceiling + watchdog — versus \"killed at 3.3GiB, empty report\" on the disk-store route. That's a 2.8× reduction in peak resident memory and comfortably inside both the 4GB cap and the 180s timeout.\n\n(The timing grep printed nothing because e_t.txt was from the previous background run whose stderr went to the same file... whatever, elapsed=7s is measured.)\n\nNow let me design the actual fix.\n\n**The gap**: under a RAM ceiling, a one-graph scan is routed through the disk-backed BadgerGraph store unconditionally. For a target whose graph fits in RAM under the ceiling — exactly the target `oneGraphSourceBytes` says needs no partitioning — the disk route's own overhead (detail buffer held as an under-estimated map, badger memtables/arenas, block cache, SST write path, mmap) exceeds the int store's entire footprint, so the memory safety stop fires before any finding is emitted. The very mode meant to keep the scan inside the ceiling is what pushes it out.\n\n**The fix (smallest)**: use the in-RAM int store for a one-graph scan under a ceiling, the same store the partition path already uses — i.e., decide the store by the same measurement that decides partitioning: the target's graph-weighted source against `scanSourceLimit`. When the target is within the one-graph limit (PlanPartitions returned no partitions), scan with `UseIntStore = true`; the disk store remains for the runs that genuinely need one huge resident graph (`needsGraph` runs where partitioning is off: `-stats`, graph-json with flags — those serialise the single store).\n\nWait — hmm. For `needsGraph` runs the scan is one graph regardless of size, so the disk store is their only bound. Keep badger there. For non-needsGraph runs: parts planned; if parts == nil (target within limit) → int store; if parts ≥ 2 → scanPartitionsCollect already sets UseIntStore=true.\n\nActually — wait. Let me re-scan the logic:\n\n```go\ncodemapParts := format == \"graph-json\" && !wantsFlags && !opts.ShowStats\nvar parts []map[string]bool\nif !needsGraph || codemapParts {\n    parts = extract.PlanPartitions(paths, opts.Excludes, scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget)\n}\nscanNow := func() {\n    if len(parts) > 1 {\n        ... scanPartitions / scanPartitionsCodemap (both set UseIntStore=true inside)\n    }\n    all, stats, graph, err = scanPathsWithProfileDemand(paths, ruleSources, prof.Name, !needsGraph, build)\n}\n```\n\nSo the non-partitioned single-graph path is `scanPathsWithProfileDemand` at line 933. With `-max-ram` set, DiskStorePath != \"\" → badger store.\n\n**Change**: when a ceiling is in force AND partition planning ran and found the target fits one graph (`parts == nil`), run that one graph on the in-RAM int store (UseIntStore), because one-graph-under-the-limit is by construction affordable in RAM. The disk store stays for `needsGraph` runs (one graph, unbounded size, needs serialisation) — and as the safety net for oversized targets whose runs skip planning.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me think again about whether it's right to keep badger for needsGraph runs. With `-stats` on openrazer under 4GB: needsGraph=true → parts=nil (not planned) → badger route → would still die at 3.3GB! The rank's fidelity proof used the plain scan (no -stats), so the primary blocked behaviour is fixed by the plain path. But \"no rule's repository-level proof can be produced\" — proofs might use -stats or other outputs? campaign-scan builds: `timeout 180 vyql scan -data <dir> --max-ram 4GB --cache off <target>` — plain. OK.\n\nBut should needsGraph runs also switch? The problem: for needsGraph, the graph must outlive the scan (serialised). A huge repo's graph genuinely needs the disk store there. But a *small* repo under the limit would also die... The same affordability argument applies: if the target is within the one-graph limit, RAM holds it. PlanPartitions could be called for needsGraph too *just for the store decision* — but its result would be ignored for partitioning (needsGraph must stay one graph). That is: plan partitions; if none are needed, use the int store; if the target is over the limit, needsGraph forces one graph → keep the disk store.\n\nHmm, that's more code. Is it justified? The gap names the repository scan (plain scan). The smallest change: decide the store by affordability wherever the one-graph path runs, which is exactly `scanPathsWithProfileDemand`'s single-graph branch... but scanPathsWithProfileDemand is called from several places (trace/query/etc. too? let me check callers).\n\nAlternative placement: inside `applyMaxRAM` we can't know the target. Inside `scanPaths...` we don't know paths' total weight... actually we DO: `scanPathsWithProfileDemand(paths, ...)` has paths. The affordability measure = PlanPartitions(paths, excludes, scanSourceLimit, ...) == nil. But PlanPartitions walks the tree (stat every file) — cheap (it's already done for non-needsGraph runs; doing it for needsGraph adds one walk... the walk is the same one the scan itself does; fine).\n\nSimplest surgical placement that fixes the gap without touching needsGraph semantics:\n\nIn `run()` (main.go, the scan command), after `parts = extract.PlanPartitions(...)`:\n\n```go\nif len(parts) <= 1 && scanSourceLimit > 0 { // fits one graph under the ceiling → hold it in RAM\n    ...force int store for this scan...\n}\n```\n\nBut applyMaxRAM sets DiskStorePath globally for the process (other subcommands?). The clean way mirroring scanPartitionsCollect: save `lowering.UseIntStore`, set true, defer restore — around the single-graph scanPathsWithProfileDemand call.\n\nHmm wait, but there's a wrinkle: with my change, for a non-needsGraph run under a ceiling, when parts==nil we use int store. But WHEN parts > 1, scanPartitions* already does it. So effectively for ALL plain scans under a ceiling the int store is used, and the badger route survives only for needsGraph single-graph runs. Is the badger route then dead for plain scans? Yes — and that's precisely the point: partitioning (RAM) covers plain scans, the disk store covers the serialise-one-huge-graph runs. Hmm, but wait — then why does the disk store exist at all...? For needsGraph runs. And as I noted, those would die on openrazer-sized+ repos... but a `-stats` run on openrazer under 4GB would die TODAY anyway (it dies even without -stats). Hmm, should I fix that too?\n\nLet me test: -stats under 4GB on combo4 currently (badger route since needsGraph skips planning).\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider. Is needsGraph really \"one graph regardless\"? With -stats, `PlanPartitions` is NOT called, so parts=nil, one graph, badger store → dies. The rank's mandated command doesn't use -stats. But \"so under the mandated 4GB cap and 180 second timeout the repository scan writes an empty report\" — the repository scan = campaign-scan's plain scan. Fix that. Making -stats runs also work would be nice but is beyond the gap; DON'T widen. But hmm — the fix's *blast radius* on scores: the store change applies to every bounded scan... the campaign measures everything again anyway.\n\nHmm, actually, hold on. Let me reconsider the alternative: fix the badger route's overhead itself (e.g., make estDetBytes accurate, shrink the detail buffer, drop the block cache when small). That keeps the architecture (disk store under ceiling) but reduces its overhead. But measured overhead is ~2.3GB on a 1.2GB workload — the write path + caches + buffers would still add GBs; you can't tune that away to zero. The int store at 1.2GB vs badger at 3.5GB+ on the SAME graph — the badger route is simply the wrong mode at this size. The affordability decision is the real fix.\n\nNow, the risk with switching plain bounded scans to the int store: a target just *under* the one-graph limit (8MB JS-equiv under 4GB) lowers to a graph of ~13MB×(8/13)≈ peak 1.9GB + binding indexes... the calibration comment says 13MB JS ≈ 3GB peak (mid-lower) — 8MB ≈ 1.9GB, plus bindings... The badger route would ALSO hold most of that in RAM (structural core is resident in both; detail spills at 512MB). So near the limit both routes are ~similar; under the limit the int store is strictly cheaper. Above the limit → partitions. Consistent.\n\nBut wait — there's an important detail: today, plain bounded scans just under the limit go badger and SURVIVE (e.g., pylib+daemon at 1.14M nodes survived at 18s). With my change they'd go int-store — cheaper, faster. Above the limit → partitioned (already int store). So the badger route for plain scans disappears entirely. Anything that could regress? The badger route exists since some version; tests? cmd tests around maxram: maxram_test.go — TestApplyMaxRAMPutsTheGraphOutsideTMPDIR expects the graph store dir on disk when -max-ram... If plain scans no longer use the graph store, that test still tests applyMaxRAM itself (flag level), not the scan flow. Let me read those tests.\n\nAlso `scancache.go` hashes scanSourceLimit/Budget into the cache key — my change doesn't alter those.\n\nAnd what about `trace`, `query`, `graph`, `resolve`, `match`, `explain` subcommands under a ceiling? They probably don't take -max-ram (only scan does? -max-ram is defined in the scan flagset — line 231 within the scan command setup. Let me verify other commands don't set DiskStorePath.)\n\nLet me look at how run() is structured to place the change, then check the maxram tests.","signature":"5d1ba7626d2f4a85a95aecc7"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"f6189413-45cd-4c3c-8507-2a4b3e0a525a","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:54:06.782Z"}
