{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917065414fb92a0fc3aaf43b0","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good — I understand the test idioms now. Before designing the change, let me quantify precisely *why* the badger route costs 2.3GB more, so the fix (and the comment justifying it) rests on measurement, not vibes. Let me compare the two 4GB runs' heap profiles. I have /tmp/heap.prof (badger route, at stop). Let me capture the int-store run's peak heap... it doesn't stop, so no auto profile. I can send SIGQUIT? No. Let me instead reason from the profile I have plus targeted numbers.\n\nFrom the badger-route heap profile at stop (in-use 1.64GB):\n- z.Calloc: 724MB (ristretto/badger C-allocated memory: block cache 256MB + ?). Actually badger v4 also Callocs for the mmap'd value log? Let's say ~724MB C-side.\n- badger skl.newArena: 83MB in-use (memtables).\n- lowering graph structures: intern 213MB + AddNode 332MB + propsWithInline 211MB + ... ≈ 900MB.\n\nFor the int-store run, the same graph is held in IntStore — the no-cap run's total peak RSS was 1259MB. So the graph + everything ≈ 1.2GB. In the badger route, the *same* graph core is resident (idx map, ids, typs, out, flow, labels... ≈ same ~900MB-1GB) PLUS:\n- g.det detail buffer — up to 512MB *estimated*; real cost is higher (estDetBytes underestimates map+struct overhead). This is detail that in the int store is in the same slices... wait, no — in the badger route the detail is ALSO in the structural core? No: in BadgerGraph, `det` holds the detail (the equivalent of IntStore's loc/region/method/... slices). So badger route = core-without-detail + det-map (up to ~512-800MB real) + badger overhead (~800MB: block cache + arenas + builders + mmap) vs intstore = core + detail-in-parallel-slices (compact).\n\nThe measured totals: 1220MB (int) vs >3500MB (badger). Difference ~2.3GB ≈ det-map real cost (~700MB?) + badger C memory 724MB + badger Go churn (arenas/builders ~400MB high-water) + GC slack from faster growth.\n\nOK. The empirical difference is what matters: for a graph that fits, the disk route multiplies peak RSS ~2.9×.\n\nNow — where exactly to make the change? Options:\n\n**Option A: in `run()`'s scanNow closure.** When `len(parts) <= 1` (no partitioning) and a ceiling is in force (`scanSourceLimit > 0`), wrap the single-graph scan in UseIntStore=true (mirroring scanPartitionsCollect's save/restore). But careful: `needsGraph` runs don't call PlanPartitions; parts stays nil; those runs would ALSO get the int store under this condition — for a huge repo, a needsGraph run would then blow memory with no spill... but wait — today those runs use badger and bounded-by-spill. Changing them to int store could make a `-stats` scan of a 100MB repo OOM-stop when previously it (slowly) completed via spill. That's a regression for needsGraph runs. So condition must be: partition planning ran AND decided one graph suffices (`!needsGraph || codemapParts` and parts == nil).\n\nHmm, but then a `-stats` scan under 4GB of openrazer still dies (badger route). The gap only names the plain repository scan; campaign-scan runs plain. Keep the change tight: only the runs that plan partitions.\n\n**Option B: inside `applyMaxRAM`** — can't; no target knowledge.\n\n**Option C: in `lowering.newGraphStore`** — no target knowledge either (hint is node count, too late).\n\nSo Option A. Concretely:\n\n```go\nscanNow := func() {\n    if len(parts) > 1 {\n        ... (unchanged; already int-store per partition)\n    }\n    // A ceiling is in force and the planner found the target fits one graph:\n    // hold that graph in RAM. The disk-backed store is for a graph that cannot\n    // fit (the -stats/graph-json runs that skip planning) — for one that does,\n    // its detail buffer, write path and cache cost more resident memory than\n    // the whole int-indexed store, and the safety stop fires before a rule runs.\n    if scanSourceLimit > 0 && (!needsGraph || codemapParts) {\n        prev := lowering.UseIntStore\n        lowering.UseIntStore = true\n        defer func() { lowering.UseIntStore = prev }()  // careful: defer inside closure runs at closure end — fine\n    }\n    all, stats, graph, err = scanPathsWithProfileDemand(...)\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, the `parts == nil` case with `!needsGraph`: PlanPartitions returns nil when the target fits OR when planning couldn't run (limit<=0 → scanSourceLimit==0 → checked). Also returns nil when it produced <2 groups. Either way \"one graph affordable\". Good.\n\nBut hold on — should the change live at the scanNow site, or is there something cleaner? Actually, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the right place is where the *decision* semantically belongs — a tiny helper next to PlanPartitions call. And the condition `(!needsGraph || codemapParts)` is exactly the condition under which parts was planned. Let me restructure minimally:\n\n```go\nvar parts []map[string]bool\nplanned := !needsGraph || codemapParts\nif planned {\n    parts = extract.PlanPartitions(...)\n}\noneGraphFitsCeiling := planned && len(parts) <= 1 && scanSourceLimit > 0\n```\n\nThen in scanNow's else branch set UseIntStore.\n\nHmm — but there's ANOTHER consumer of the single-graph path: the `collect` for sync (graphsync) — line 933 is the single call. OK.\n\nWait, one more consideration: `trace`, `query`, `graph`, `resolve`, `match`, `explain` commands — do they set -max-ram? Let me check which commands define maxRAM (line 231 is inside the scan command's flagset — `fs` for scan). Let me confirm no other command sets DiskStorePath.","signature":"e16b54f98f4c451790fe9755"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"e99f2364-8343-477d-b86d-1b98ecd5e10b","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:54:42.237Z"}
