{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917065444cc18a410745b4f83","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Only `scan` arms `-max-ram`. Good — the change is scoped to the scan command's `run()`.\n\nNow let me think about **which shape the failing test should take**. The requirement: \"A Go test that fails without your change and passes after.\"\n\nThe behaviour: under a RAM ceiling, a scan whose target fits the one-graph limit must hold its graph in RAM (int store) rather than routing through the disk-backed store whose overhead crosses the safety stop. Test options:\n\n1. **Unit test at the decision level**: refactor the store decision into a small function `oneGraphHoldsInRAM(planned bool, parts []map[string]bool) bool` and test it — weak, tests the wiring not the behaviour.\n\n2. **Behavioural test in cmd/vyql**: run a real scan under `applyMaxRAM(\"4GB\")` over a fixture small enough to fit one graph, and assert... what? That it completes? Today it also completes (small fixture doesn't hit the stop). Need to observe *which store* was used. `scanPathsWithProfileDemand` returns `usg.Store` — the test can type-assert the returned store: `*usg.IntStore` vs `*usg.BadgerGraph`. That's a direct, honest assertion of the behaviour: under a ceiling, a target inside the one-graph limit is scanned on the in-RAM store; the disk-backed store remains for runs that skip planning (needsGraph). Fails before (store is *BadgerGraph), passes after.\n\nThat's a clean failing test in the package I change (cmd/vyql). It needs:\n- `cacheHome(t)` + memory-limit save/restore (idiom exists in maxram_test.go).\n- `applyMaxRAM(\"8GB\")` to arm the ceiling and budgets.\n- a fixture that (a) produces a graph, (b) is under oneGraphSourceBytes(8GB) — any tiny fixture is.\n- rules from loadRules(\"\") — needs the data dir (VYQL_HOME) as other tests do (partition_test.go uses loadRules without data? `loadRules(\"\")` — the tests run with VYQL_HOME set by CI/make; locally I'll need to fetch definitions or set VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/defs. Hmm — partition_test.go calls loadRules(\"\") and scanPathsWithProfileDemand directly, and CI passes -data/$VYQL_HOME. For `go test` locally I need VYQL_HOME pointing at the definitions. The repo fetches free definitions into ./vyql; here I can set VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/defs (top dir has bindings/ etc.). Let me check datadir.Lookup to be sure what it accepts.)\n\nAlso assert the counterpart: a `needsGraph`-shaped run (or rather: the disk store stays when planning is skipped)? Careful — needsGraph runs go through run(), not through scanPathsWithProfileDemand directly. My test calls scanPathsWithProfileDemand the way run() does; the decision logic must therefore live where the test can drive it. If the decision lives inline in run()'s scanNow closure, a unit test can't reach it. So either:\n- put the decision in a helper the closure calls, and test through the helper + one integration-ish assertion; or\n- test through `run(...)` itself? run() prints to stdout; it's called by tests? Let me check whether existing tests call run() directly... let me search.","signature":"59d312c386054dcda612b752"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"916f921c-0bfd-4a8c-98ef-d40e47b7668b","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:54:57.716Z"}
