{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917065458f962d589cdcc4d7d","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Tests do call `run(...)` directly (cli_test.go). So a behavioural test through `run()` is idiomatic here. But `run()` doesn't return the store — the store choice is observable via... hmm. `run()` prints findings; the store type isn't visible in output.\n\nTwo-layer test plan:\n1. **The failing test** (behaviour): a bounded scan of a fixture that fits one graph must complete on the in-RAM store — observable by type-asserting the store returned from the single-graph scan path under an armed ceiling. But the decision is made inside run()'s closure...\n\nAlternative: make the decision *inside* `scanPathsWithProfileDemand` itself! Look at its signature: `scanPathsWithProfileDemand(paths, ruleSources, profileName, pruneBindings, build)`. It has `paths`. The single-graph call sites are: run()'s closure (scan under ceiling or no ceiling), and the tests/benchmark. If the store decision lived in `scanPathsWithProfileDemand`:\n\n```go\n// inside, before BuildGraph:\nif DiskStorePath set && target fits one graph under scanSourceLimit → UseIntStore for this build\n```\n\nHmm, but scanPartitionsCollect calls scanPathsWithProfileDemand per partition — with `Only` set; each partition is by construction under the limit, so the logic would naturally give int store for partitions too (it already forces it globally — I could then even simplify that away, but don't touch what works).\n\nBut careful — `scanPathsWithProfileDemand` is also used by needsGraph runs (from run()'s closure, when parts weren't planned). If the decision is inside it, needsGraph runs under a ceiling ALSO get the int store when the target fits one graph — which is fine memory-wise (if it fits, int store is cheaper) — and when it does NOT fit (over the limit), the disk store stays. Wait, that's actually *better*: the affordability logic applies uniformly; the disk store kicks in exactly when the graph can't be held in RAM under the ceiling, regardless of needsGraph. And for needsGraph runs over the limit: needsGraph runs skip PlanPartitions so parts==nil and they take the single-graph path — with decision inside scanPathsWithProfileDemand, an over-limit needsGraph run keeps the badger store (since target doesn't fit). \n\nSo the cleanest formulation:\n\nIn `scanPathsWithProfileDemand` (or just inside run()'s closure around the single-graph call — but tests reach the former more naturally):\n\n```go\n// A ceiling is in force: decide whether this one graph belongs in RAM. The\n// disk-backed store exists for a graph the ceiling cannot hold...\n```\n\nWhere's the \"does the target fit one graph\" measure? `extract.PlanPartitions(paths, excludes, scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget) == nil`. But scanPathsWithProfileDemand has opts.Excludes. Planning walks the whole tree — the scan itself walks it again; PlanPartitions is cheap (stat + classify). But hmm — is calling PlanPartitions inside scanPathsWithProfileDemand safe for all callers? benchmark_test calls it with roots; scanSourceLimit would be 0 in tests (no ceiling) → skip planning entirely (`limit<=0 → nil`). Only when a ceiling is armed does the extra walk happen. Good.\n\nWait, but there's double-walking in run(): PlanPartitions called at line 920, then again inside the single-graph scan. That's one extra stat-walk of the target — cheap relative to the scan. Acceptable? The partitioned path avoids it (parts>1 branch). Could thread the decision down from run() to avoid re-planning... but keeping the decision local to the single-graph builder is simpler and self-contained. Hmm, but \"smallest change\" — an extra full tree walk per bounded scan... PlanPartitions stats every file and classifies extensions; for a 100k-file repo that's ~100k stats ≈ well under a second. The partition planner already does exactly this walk in the same run. I'll accept the duplicate walk; OR I can pass the decision from run() where planning already happened.\n\nHmm. Let me think about which is less invasive and most testable:\n\n**Design: `scanPathsWithProfileDemand` asks a helper when a ceiling is armed.**\n\n```go\nfunc scanPathsWithProfileDemand(paths []string, ruleSources []parser.V2DefinitionSource, profileName string, pruneBindings bool, build extract.Options) (...) {\n    ...\n    restoreStore := holdGraphInRAMIfItFits(paths, build.Excludes)   // no-op without a ceiling\n    defer restoreStore()\n    g, stats, err := extract.BuildGraph(...)\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```go\n// holdGraphInRAMIfItFits decides the store for ONE resident graph under a RAM ceiling.\n// ...\nfunc holdGraphInRAMIfItFits(paths []string, excludes extract.Excludes) func() {\n    if lowering.DiskStorePath == \"\" || scanSourceLimit <= 0 {\n        return func() {}\n    }\n    if extract.PlanPartitions(paths, excludes, scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget) != nil {\n        return func() {} // over the one-graph limit: the disk-backed store is the bound\n    }\n    prev := lowering.UseIntStore\n    lowering.UseIntStore = true\n    return func() { lowering.UseIntStore = prev }\n}\n```\n\nWait — but there's a subtlety with scanPartitionsCollect: it sets UseIntStore=true around the whole loop; the per-partition calls to scanPathsWithProfileDemand would call holdGraphInRAMIfItFits — each partition fits (they're sized under budget ≤ limit) → sets UseIntStore true, restores to... prev=true (set by the loop). Fine, no interference.\n\nAnd the graph *release* — with int store the graph is Go heap; scanPartitionsCollect does debug.FreeOSMemory() after closing each partition. For the single-graph path the store lives until output. Fine.\n\nThere's one more consumer of BuildGraph with DiskStorePath set: none outside scan (only scan sets -max-ram). And `trace`/`query`/etc. don't. OK.\n\nNow, the test that fails before / passes after. In cmd/vyql:\n\n```go\nfunc TestBoundedScanHoldsAOneGraphTargetInRAM(t *testing.T) {\n    cacheHome(t)\n    prevLimit := debug.SetMemoryLimit(-1)\n    t.Cleanup(func() { debug.SetMemoryLimit(prevLimit) })\n    cleanup := applyMaxRAM(\"8GB\")\n    t.Cleanup(cleanup)\n\n    dir := partitionFixture(t, 3) // small: far under the one-graph limit\n    rules, err := loadRules(\"\")\n    ...\n    _, _, g, err := scanPathsWithProfileDemand([]string{dir}, rules, \"\", true, extract.Options{})\n    if err != nil { t.Fatalf(...) }\n    if g == nil { t.Fatal(\"no graph\") }\n    if _, ok := g.(*usg.BadgerGraph); ok {\n        t.Errorf(\"a target that fits one graph under the ceiling was scanned on the disk-backed store, whose detail buffer, write path and cache cost more resident memory than the whole in-RAM store — the memory safety stop fires before a rule runs\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAnd a companion asserting the disk store still arms for a target over the limit (so the fix didn't just disable badger): scanSourceLimit tiny → PlanPartitions returns parts → but wait, over-the-limit in scanPathsWithProfileDemand keeps badger — the test can force scanSourceLimit=1 (or a big fixture). Hmm: set scanSourceLimit/Budget small, scan a fixture bigger than that, assert store IS *BadgerGraph. That guards the other direction.\n\nWait — careful: with scanSourceLimit tiny and the test calling scanPathsWithProfileDemand directly, PlanPartitions != nil → keeps disk store. Good.\n\nBut hold on: is asserting on store type too implementation-flavoured? The genuinely behavioural assertion would be memory — but a test that measures RSS is flaky. The store-type assertion is the direct observable of the decision, and the decision IS the behaviour (\"the bounded one-graph scan holds its graph in RAM\"). The commit comment + evidence carries the memory measurement. I think type assertion is right, and there's precedent style-wise (badgergraph_test.go type-asserts store behaviours).\n\nHmm, wait — actually let me reconsider placing the decision inside scanPathsWithProfileDemand vs run(). One more wrinkle: `scanPathsWithProfileDemand` is called by run()'s closure for EVERY non-partitioned scan — including **unbounded** scans (DiskStorePath == \"\" → helper no-ops). Good.\n\nAlso `sync` runs (graphsync) go through run() → same path. Fine.\n\nNow — is `UseIntStore` the right lever, or should the store decision rather live in `lowering.newGraphStore` with a new knob? UseIntStore is exactly \"force the int store\" and applyMaxRAM's own fallback uses it for exactly this semantics (\"lower-footprint in-RAM store\"). Reusing it keeps the knob count unchanged.\n\nOne more consideration: with UseIntStore forced, the transient **body spool** that applyMaxRAM installs (parsecache.OpenTransient) still applies — that's good (it bounds frontend body memory) and unchanged.\n\nAnd `lowering.DiskCacheBytes/DiskDetailBuf` stay set but unused by the int store — harmless.\n\nWhat about `armMemoryWatch` + `goHeapMemoryLimit` staying armed? Yes — the ceiling still applies; if the target genuinely doesn't fit even in RAM... wait, for an over-the-limit target the scan is partitioned (plain scans). For needsGraph over-the-limit → badger. Consistent.\n\nEdge: `-max-ram` given AND `needsGraph` AND target over limit → badger store → today's behaviour. Unchanged. Good.\n\nEdge: plain scan, target over limit → partitions → int store per partition (today's behaviour, unchanged).\n\nEdge: plain scan, target under limit → int store (CHANGED — the fix).\n\nNow — what about **correctness of findings**? IntStore vs BadgerGraph: \"It implements usg.Store identically to InMemStore (verified by the shared suite)\". BadgerGraph had a subtle props round-trip bug once (comment at line 77-79). Findings could differ between stores? The badgergraph comment says they made them agree. With my change, bounded scans (int store) now use the SAME store as unbounded scans (int store) — so bounded and unbounded scans agree *more* than before. That's a precision/recall consideration for the benchmarks: OWASP benchmarks run unbounded → int store → unchanged. Bounded corpus runs previously badger → now int; if the stores disagree anywhere, scores could move. The review measures everything; my job is to note it.\n\nLet me now also confirm the claim \"detail buffer underestimation\" isn't the primary driver vs simply \"badger costs a lot\" — actually for the fix it doesn't matter: the measured totals (1220 vs >3500) justify the route change regardless of the breakdown. But let me grab one more data point to make the comment honest: measure the badger-route run's peak if allowed to finish (the 6GB run: what was its peak RSS?). 6GB cap on combo4: detail buf 768MB, stop 5.2GB. Let me sample its peak to report \"the same scan on the disk-backed route peaks at X\".","signature":"ec4d456eb4ad443c9165918a"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"1e1d63c8-885f-41a2-89c8-a4443aa770e4","timestamp":"2026-09-16T22:55:38.310Z"}
