{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609170656138779663f88b74ebe","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Confirmed: the disk-backed route peaks at **4626 MiB** for a graph the int store holds at **1220 MiB** — a 3.8× multiplier. Under the mandated 4GB ceiling (stop at 3.5GB) it can never finish; it died at 3.3GiB.\n\nSummary of measurements (openrazer pylib+daemon+scripts, 90 files, 21.5k lines, 1.29M-node graph):\n- no ceiling, int store: peak 1259 MiB, ~9s, 53 findings ≥high.\n- 4GB ceiling, disk-backed store: **killed by the memory safety stop at 3.3GiB, empty report** (reproduced identically 3×; batch-base definitions hit the same stop — definition-independent).\n- 4GB ceiling, in-RAM store (fallback path forced): peak 1220 MiB, 7s, 53 findings — completes.\n- 6GB ceiling, disk-backed store: completes but peaks at 4626 MiB.\n\nNow implement. The change in `cmd/vyql/main.go`:\n\n1. A helper next to the max-ram machinery:\n\n```go\n// holdOneGraphInRAM decides the store for a scan that builds ONE resident graph under a\n// RAM ceiling. The disk-backed store is that graph's bound when the ceiling cannot hold\n// it; for a target the ceiling comfortably holds, the store costs more than the graph:\n// its detail buffer, write path and caches are resident alongside a structural core that\n// never leaves RAM, and a multi-package target that lowers to a million nodes crosses the\n// safety threshold before the first rule runs — an empty report from a scan the ceiling\n// could easily afford. The partition planner's own measure decides which case this is:\n// a target PlanPartitions leaves unpartitioned is a target one graph holds, and the\n// int-indexed in-RAM store is the cheaper way to hold it. Over the limit the scan is\n// partitioned (each partition in RAM) or, for a run that must serialise one graph\n// (-stats, graph-json), the disk store remains the bound.\nfunc holdOneGraphInRAM(paths []string, excludes extract.Excludes) func() {\n\tif lowering.DiskStorePath == \"\" || scanSourceLimit <= 0 {\n\t\treturn func() {}\n\t}\n\tif extract.PlanPartitions(paths, excludes, scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget) != nil {\n\t\treturn func() {}\n\t}\n\tprev := lowering.UseIntStore\n\tlowering.UseIntStore = true\n\treturn func() { lowering.UseIntStore = prev }\n}\n```\n\n2. Wire it in `scanPathsWithProfileDemand` before BuildGraph:\n\n```go\n\tbuild.Sync = syncCollector\n\trestoreStore := holdOneGraphInRAM(paths, build.Excludes)\n\tg, stats, err := extract.BuildGraph(paths, extract.SharedDeltaCache(), build)\n\trestoreStore()   // hmm — when to restore?\n```\n\nWait — restore timing. UseIntStore is consulted at `newGraphStore(hint)` during BuildGraph (at lowering start). After BuildGraph returns, the store exists; restoring right after BuildGraph is fine and keeps the global perturbation window minimal. scanPartitionsCollect sets it around its whole loop — because it makes multiple builds. Here: set before BuildGraph, restore right after. If BuildGraph errors, still restore (defer or explicit). Let me write:\n\n```go\n\trestoreStore := holdOneGraphInRAM(paths, build.Excludes)\n\tg, stats, err := extract.BuildGraph(paths, extract.SharedDeltaCache(), build)\n\trestoreStore()\n```\n\nRestore even on err — yes, it runs unconditionally there.\n\nHmm wait — one question: does anything LATER in the pipeline consult UseIntStore again for the same graph (e.g. incremental lowering creating more stores)? LowerIncremental also calls newGraphStore. BuildGraph calls either LowerIncremental or LowerTypedDeferred — one store per BuildGraph. Restoring after BuildGraph returns is correct.\n\nBut — `scanPartitionsCollect` sets `lowering.UseIntStore = true` around its loop AND calls scanPathsWithProfileDemand per partition; with my change each partition call also sets/restores around its own BuildGraph: prev captured = true (loop's value), restored = true. No interference. And my holdOneGraphIn