e resident memory than the in-RAM store it exists to spare\")\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nHmm — does scanPathsWithProfileDemand return the store as usg.Store holding *usg.IntStore? BuildGraph → LowerTypedDeferred → newGraphStore → *usg.IntStore. Yes. Need import of usg.\n\nTest 2 (guard the other direction): a target over the limit keeps the disk-backed bound:\n```go\nfunc TestBoundedScanSpillsAGraphTheCeilingCannotHold(t *testing.T) {\n\t... applyMaxRAM(\"8GB\"); then squeeze the limit:\n\tprevLimit, prevBudget := scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget\n\tt.Cleanup(restore); scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget = 1, 1   // every target is over it\n\tdir := partitionFixture(t, 3)\n\t... scanPathsWithProfileDemand ...\n\tif _, ok := g.(*usg.BadgerGraph); !ok {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"a target over the one-graph limit lost the disk-backed bound\")\n\t}\n}\n```\nWait — PlanPartitions with limit=1,budget=1: total > 1 → groups by path order with budget 1 → each file its own group → ≥2 groups → non-nil → helper no-op → badger. Yes.\n\nHmm, one caution on test 2: the badger store creation needs a writable cache dir — cacheHome(t) provides. And closing: the returned store should be closed by the test (usg.Close(g)) so the temp dir is removed; applyMaxRAM cleanup removes the parent. partition_test doesn't close stores from scanPathsWithProfileDemand (they leak into the test process; fine, but badger holds a dir under cacheHome's tempdir which t.TempDir() cleans... if not closed, RemoveAll might fail on open files on some systems; better to close: `t.Cleanup(func(){ _ = usg.Close(g) })`).\n\nAlso — the fixture: partitionFixture writes express-style JS handlers. loadRules(\"\") requires definitions (VYQL_HOME or -data). CI provides; locally I'll set VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/defs. Hmm wait — cli tests already depend on definitions being present, so the suite has that expectation baked in. Fine.\n\nDo I need findings from the fixture? No — only the store type. But the graph must be non-nil: `len(prog.Modules) > 0` — 3 JS files produce modules. BuildGraph returns (g, stats, err); if all files parse. JS fixture from partition_test is proven.\n\n5. ALSO: the *evidence* the task cites is a real repository scan. The end-to-end proof: rerun the mandated campaign-scan on the openrazer checkout with the patched binary and show 53+ findings (and specifically that VYQL-MEM-104 fires with the rank's definitions — actually the rank's binding lives in the rank's tree; the gap is engine-only, so my proof is: whole-repo scan now completes under 4GB/180s. The rank's own re-proof comes later.)\n\nNow, one more scan of concerns:\n\n**Concurrency**: `lowering.UseIntStore` is a package global read at newGraphStore during BuildGraph; scans are sequential in-process. Tests could run in parallel? Go tests in one package run sequentially unless t.Parallel(). Fine.\n\n**Determinism of PlanPartitions vs the scan's own file set**: PlanPartitions(paths, excludes, ...) uses the same excludes; returns nil exactly when run() got nil. Consistent decision. One asymmetry: run() plans with `opts.Excludes` (scanRunOptions.Excludes) — same value passed into build.Excludes? In run(): `build := extract.Options{BindingOverlay: ..., Excludes: opts.Excludes}` and planning uses `opts.Excludes`. In scanPathsWithProfileDemand I have `build.Excludes` — same. Good.\n\n**Cache-key concern**: scancache.go hashes scanSourceLimit/Budget — unchanged. The whole-scan cache stores findings; with the store change, findings could differ between cached (badger-era) and fresh (int-era) results for bounded cached scans... The campaign runs `-cache off`. Persistent caches from older versions may hold badger-produced findings; store-equivalence (verified by shared suite) makes this a non-issue in principle. Not my lane to bump a cache version... actually hmm, should I? scanFingerprint includes... let me check whether the store choice is in the fingerprint. If a user has a populated cache from the previous binary, replaying it 