{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609170706064abc2f5c2d4c4433","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Lint clean. While the full suite runs, let me re-examine my change once more for correctness concerns:\n\n1. **Double tree walk**: run() calls PlanPartitions (line ~920), then the single-graph scanPathsWithProfileDemand calls holdOneGraphInRAM → PlanPartitions again. For a bounded scan of a huge repo (over limit), run() plans partitions and takes the parts>1 branch — the inner helper is never reached (partitions loop). For a bounded scan under the limit: run() plans (nil), single-graph scan plans again (nil) — two walks. Each walk stats every file. For the openrazer repo: negligible. For a 100k-file repo: ~100k extra stats once. Acceptable? The scan itself parses thousands of files for seconds-to-minutes; stats are microseconds each. Fine.\n\n2. **`syncCollector`**: runs with graphsync still go through run() → single-graph path — with ceiling armed and target fitting → int store. Does graphsync work over IntStore? It collects during lowering (opts.Sync.CollectGraph(g, ...)) — the collector works on any usg.Store. The badger path was chosen for memory, not correctness. Fine.\n\n3. **neighbours**: `-stats` runs (needsGraph) under a ceiling: PlanPartitions is NOT called by run()... wait, my helper calls PlanPartitions *inside* scanPathsWithProfileDemand regardless of needsGraph! So a `-stats` run under 4GB on openrazer: helper plans → nil (fits) → **int store** → completes! That's actually a *bonus* fix (the -stats route previously died). And a -stats run on an over-limit repo: helper plans → non-nil → badger → spill (unchanged). This is exactly the right semantics in both directions. \n\nWait, but hold on — there's an inefficiency: for an over-limit target with `-stats`, the helper's PlanPartitions walk is wasted (result discarded, one graph built anyway). That's the price of deciding locally; one stat