{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917071018502b854e212a44df","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_349d2f49ff4247fd8aff67d2","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"git commit -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nengine: hold a one-graph bounded scan's graph in RAM when the ceiling fits it\n\n-max-ram armed the disk-backed BadgerGraph store for every scan it\nbounded. For a target whose graph fits in the ceiling, the store costs\nmore than the graph it spares: its detail buffer, write path and caches\nsit resident alongside a structural core that never leaves RAM in\neither store, so a multi-package repository that lowers to a\nmillion-odd nodes peaked several times its in-RAM footprint (measured\n4626MiB through the disk store against 1220MiB in RAM on the same\nrepository) and the memory safety stop ended the scan before the first\nrule ran — an empty report from a 4GB ceiling the scan easily afforded.\n\nThe partition planner's own affordability measure now decides the\nstore: a target PlanPartitions leaves unpartitioned is a target one\ngraph holds, and that graph is built on the int-indexed in-RAM store —\nthe same store a partitioned scan already builds per partition. A\ngraph the ceiling cannot hold still spills: a target over the\none-graph limit is partitioned, and a run that must serialise one\ngraph (-stats, graph-json with review flags) keeps the disk-backed\nbound. Unbounded scans are untouched; a scan whose cache directories\nare unwritable keeps its existing in-RAM fallback.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\n)\" && git log -1 --stat | head -20","description":"Commit the fix with attribution"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"3d499fab-e518-4b3c-b897-a30d373e49c5","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:10:22.736Z"}
