{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_28d204cb7e184bcabeb683be","type":"tool_result","content":"ci-fix-2020-2029\nci-fix-2030-2039\nci-fix-2120-2129\nci-fix-2410-2419\nci-fix-2450-2459\nci-fix-2460-2469\nci-fix-2520-2529\nci-fix-2590-2599\nci-fix-2620-2629\nci-fix-2680-2689\nci-fix-2730-2739\nci-fix-2880-2889\nci-fix-2910-2919\nci-fix-2990-2999\nci-fix-2999-3008\nengine-a-c-cast-expression-lowers-to-a-pass-through-nir-thru-that-d\nengine-a-c-cast-expression-lowers-to-a-pass-through-nir-thru-that-d.answer.json\nengine-a-c-cast-expression-lowers-to-a-pass-through-nir-thru-that-d.task.json\nengine-a-call-whose-callee-is-a-plain-identifier-bound-by-a-const-i\nengine-a-call-whose-callee-is-a-plain-identifier-bound-by-a-const-i.answer.json\nengine-a-call-whose-callee-is-a-plain-identifier-bound-by-a-const-i.task.json\nengine-a-check-does-not-discharge-a-later-read-of-the-same-variable\nengine-a-check-does-not-discharge-a-later-read-of-the-same-variable.answer.json\nengine-a-check-does-not-discharge-a-later-read-of-the-same-variable.task.json\nengine-a-check-or-escape-a-javascript-function-delegates-to-a-sibli\nengine-a-check-or-escape-a-javascript-function-delegates-to-a-sibli.answer.json\nengine-a-check-or-escape-a-javascript-function-delegates-to-a-sibli.task.json\nengine-a-java-class-s-own-annotations-are-not-carried-in-the-class-\nengine-a-java-class-s-own-annotations-are-not-carried-in-the-class-.answer.json\nengine-a-java-class-s-own-annotations-are-not-carried-in-the-class-.task.json\n---\n{\n  \"rank\": 2794,\n  \"cve\": \"CVE-2022-23467\",\n  \"status\": \"ATTENTION\",\n  \"evidence\": \"CVE-2022-23467 (openrazer 3.5.0, CWE-125/CWE-805, malicious-USB-device vector): razer_get_usb_response (driver/razercommon.c) memcpy's the device-chosen buffer into the caller's razer_report wholesale, so the record's own payload-length field answers whatever the device wrote, and razer_attr_read_dpi_stages (driver/razermouse_driver.c) bounds its cursor walk with args + 4 > response.arguments + response.data_size while memcpy()ing four bytes out of the cursor each round -- 255 against the declared unsigned char arguments[80], whose invariant the header itself states ('Data Size is the size of payload, cannot be greater than 80') and no code enforces. Fix 33aa7f07 ('driver: Sanitize data_size value returned from devices') clamps response_report->data_size = ARRAY_SIZE(response_report->arguments) where the report is filled. Shipped: new concept code.ReportLengthFieldUnclampedToDeclaredArray with review entry and rule VYQL-MEM-104 (vypr.memory, severity high, confidenceFloor medium, CWE_125/CWE_805), plus one leaf file-scoped C binding vyql/bindings/c/razer_report_data_size_unclamped.vyql: the walk's stated end is the record's own field pair (binary response.arguments+response.data_size, cursor shape ID+NUM>ID.FIELD+ID.FIELD and its mirror), the response arrives through razer_send_payload -- the library's own response path, which gates the pairing to openrazer and its forks -- and the label clears when the same file clamps the field to the capacity in the fix's own ARRAY_SIZE spelling, in a literal guard on the record's response spelling, or as a bare clamp assignment. A taint route was built and measured before falling back to a match witness: a temporary source on usb_control_msg's data argument produced two code.NetworkData sources and vyql trace -from code.NetworkData -to code.IntegerSizeArithmeticSink reported both dead-ending immediately (memcpy is not a value propagator on this engine and the razer_send_payload -> razer_get_report -> razer_get_usb_response chain is not traced), so the existing taint rules VYQL-NUM-003/VYQL-MEM-003/VYQL-MEM-005 cannot judge this and the temporary binding was removed. Measured with campaign-scan on repo/driver (13 C files) at timeout 180 / --max-ram 4GB / --cache off: 105 findings at the parent 8f50fda and 105 at the fix 33aa7f07 with the batch base's definitions and no MEM-104 anywhere; with this change 106 at both revisions, the delta exactly one new VYQL-MEM-104 at razermouse_driver.c with the same fingerprint ff9c156c2b773d4c on both sides -- the fix's clamp sits in razercommon.c, another translation unit, and a file-scoped view cannot see it, which is the binding's documented residual. Separation matrix on isolated snippets: the vulnerable form fires; the fix as shipped (WARN_ONCE + clamp in the same file), a consumer-side literal guard and clamp, a bare ARRAY_SIZE clamp assignment, a reversed literal guard, and the same walk stated against arguments + ARRAY_SIZE(arguments) all clear; a literal-80 exclusion needle was dropped because the engine's value-flow-widened operand matching lands it on the walk's own bound and clears the vulnerable form with it. Spec vyql/tests/cve003/cve_rank2794_openrazer_response_data_size_unclamped.test.vyql, four cases on the real signatures and the real interprocedural dataflow (razer_send_control_msg -> usb_control_msg, the memcpy into the out-param, razer_send_payload's return by value, the consumer's walk): expect on the parent's code, reject on the fix as shipped with every call kept, on a consumer-side clamp, and on the capacity-stated bound. Full TestVyqlSpecs green at tip (9370 specs from 2695 files, no other spec moved); with the spec copied into the batch base tree the expect case fails there, so the change detects its own case and the base does not. The block that holds the rank: the fidelity proof needs a scan of the whole clone, and campaign-scan over repo/ under the mandated limits stops at the engine's memory safety threshold (3.3 GiB of its 4GB ceiling) with an empty report on every attempt, text and SARIF alike; with the ceiling raised the scan still runs past 13 minutes. The wall is definition-independent -- the batch base's definitions hit the same stop in 7 seconds -- and sits in the Python graph over pylib + daemon + scripts (90 files, 21.5k lines; each subtree alone scans in seconds, only the combination explodes), which is the engine's behaviour and outside this rank's lane, so it is declared as the engine gap and the repository-level proof is recorded from the driver subtree. No OWASP or port corpus is present in this environment, so the protected benchmarks were not runnable here; the binding is C-only and self-gated on openrazer's own razer_send_payload and response.arguments+response.data_size vocabulary, which no other clone carries. make load green, hygiene clean except the harness's own snapshot files, coverage failing only on rank 2794's missing ledger row which integration writes. The clone at repo/ is left at the fix commit 33aa7f07 as instructed.\",\n  \"blast_radius\": \"shared\",\n  \"files\": [\n    \"vyql/bindings/c/razer_report_data_size_unclamped.vyql\",\n    \"vyql/ontology/concepts/code/ReportLengthFieldUnclampedToDeclaredArray.vyql\",\n    \"vyql/review/concepts/review/ReportLengthFieldUnclampedToDeclaredArray.vyql\",\n    \"vyql/packs/memory/vypr/memory/ReportLengthFieldUnclampedToDeclaredArray.vyql\"\n  ],\n  \"spec\": \"vyql/tests/cve003/cve_rank2794_openrazer_response_data_size_unclamped.test.vyql\",\n  \"proof_shape\": {\n    \"rule\": \"VYQL-MEM-104\",\n    \"source\": \"none: usb_control_msg carries no source binding and the engine does not propagate taint through memcpy into the response record (vyql trace dead-ends both sources), so the finding is a match witness on the walking file\",\n    \"sink\": \"code.ReportLengthFieldUnclampedToDeclaredArray\",\n    \"witness_kind\": \"match witness\",\n    \"missing\": []\n  },\n  \"engine_gap\": \"the engine's memory safety stop ends a whole-repository scan before any finding is emitted when the Python lowering and graph construction over multi-package code exceeds it, so under the mandated 4GB cap and 180 second timeout the repository scan writes an empty report and no rule's repository-level proof can be produced\"\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null