{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260917071041b41cfd42a694454a","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Tree is clean except the harness leftovers (not mine to commit — the task said leftovers are from dead runs; `git clean -fd` is safe in this worktree, but harness.err/harness.log were present at start and are the harness's own snapshot files; the rank-2794 verdict mentions \"hygiene clean except the harness's own snapshot files\" — so leaving them untracked is the established convention. Actually, should I clean them? The instruction says leftovers in the worktree are from dead runs and `git reset --hard && git clean -fd` is safe here — but these were present at session start, described as harness's own files. The rank verdict treats \"hygiene clean except the harness's own snapshot files\" as acceptable. I'll leave them — deleting the harness's live files could interfere with the harness reading my verdict. Safer to leave untracked.\n\nNow write the verdict. Content per the established schema and my verified evidence:\n\n**gap**: \"the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan\"\n**status**: \"DONE\"\n**commit\"/\"commits\"**: dae0d186c59ba6dfbacdd01b8b8334af4e6a9811\n**branch**: engine-work/the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan\n**base**: 522a598a1dc547c02c2173af9b536d000dbe06bd\n**changed_files**: [\"cmd/vyql/main.go\", \"cmd/vyql/maxram_test.go\", \"CHANGELOG.md\"]\n**blocked_ranks**: [2794]\n**engine_gap**: null\n\n**what_was_missing**: The gap behaviour: applyMaxRAM armed the disk-backed BadgerGraph store (lowering.DiskStorePath) for EVERY bounded scan; a single-graph scan whose target fits the ceiling then pays the store's overhead on top of the structural core that never leaves RAM. Detail: partitioned scans already set UseIntStore=true per partition (scanPartitionsCollect), and the unwritable-cache fallback set UseIntStore=true; but the plain one-graph bounded scan — which the partition planner itself deems affordable (PlanPartitions returns nil) — was routed through BadgerGraph. On rank 2794's openrazer clone: whole-repo campaign-scan (timeout 180, --max-ram 4GB, --cache off) died at the 3.3GiB safety stop in ~8s with exit 1 and an empty report; the trigger is the Python graph over pylib+daemon+scripts (90 files, additive ~1.29M nodes); lowering itself is 2s and additive — the blowup is the store, measured 4626MiB peak through BadgerGraph vs 1220–1259MiB through IntStore (3.8×) on the same target; the stop fired during bindings, just before finishing. So no rule's repository-level proof could be produced (rank 2794's verdict records exactly this wall and had to prove from the driver subtree alone).\n\n**what_changed**: holdOneGraphInRAM in cmd/vyql/main.go — decides the store by the same affordability measure that decides partitioning: if a disk store is armed, a source limit is in force, and PlanPartitions(paths, excludes, scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget) == nil (the target fits one graph), set lowering.UseIntStore=true around extract.BuildGraph and restore after; returns a no-op restore otherwise. Wired in scanPathsWithProfileDemand before BuildGraph. applyMaxRAM's doc comment updated to point at it. Smallest change: 3 lines of decision + wiring; no vyql/ edits; no store implementation changed; unbounded scans untouched (DiskStorePath==\"\" → no-op); over-limit needsGraph runs (-stats, graph-json with review flags) keep the disk store; partitioned scans unchanged.\n\n**test**: TestBoundedOneGraphScanHoldsItsGraphInRAM (cmd/vyql/maxram_test.go) — arms applyMaxRAM(\"8GB\"), scans partitionFixture(t, 3) via scanPathsWithProfileDemand, asserts the store is NOT *usg.BadgerGraph. Verified RED at base 522a598a1d (fails: \"a target inside the one-graph limit was scanned on the disk-backed store\") and GREEN at HEAD. Companion TestBoundedScanSpillsAGraphTheCeilingCannotHold squeezes scanSourceLimit/Budget to 1 and asserts the store IS *usg.BadgerGraph — passes before and after, guarding the disk store's role. Suites: go test -count=1 ./... exit 0 (30 packages ok, incl. TestVyqlSpecs) with VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/defs/vyql; gofmt clean; golangci-lint v2.12.1 0 issues. Note about VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/defs failing identically at base (pre-existing duplicate vyql/ subdir artifact).\n\n**verification**: \n- Reproduction at base: whole openrazer clone (repo/ at fix 33aa7f07), campaign-scan repo/ --data-dir <defs> → memory stop at 3.3GiB in ~8s, exit 1, empty report — identical with the batch base's definitions, so definition-independent.\n- Root cause by measurement: per-subtree scans seconds; pylib+daemon+scripts combination explodes; lowering node counts additive and ~2s; forcing the in-RAM fallback (unwritable cache dirs) made the SAME 4GB scan complete → the disk store is the 3.8× (4626MiB vs 1220MiB peak, sampled /proc VmRSS).\n- After fix, mandated campaign-scan on whole clone at fix 33aa7f07: exit 3, 208 findings, ~8s, peak 1341MiB against the 3328MiB threshold; at parent 8f50fda: 208 findings, exactly one fingerprint differs (087c951d764434db → 2aa47a94d641bcfa) — repository-level proof now producible. driver subtree alone: 105 findings both revisions, matching the rank's recorded base number.\n- Bonuses: bounded -stats and graph-json runs of fitting targets now complete too.\n- Benchmarks: OWASP corpora not present in this environment, so the benchmark jobs were not runnable — said plainly. Full go test ./... green.\n\n**blast_radius**:\n- Every bounded (-max-ram) scan that builds one graph for a target inside the one-graph limit now uses IntStore instead of BadgerGraph. That is the intended change and it is findings-neutral (store implements the same usg.Store interface, verified by the shared suite; 208 findings identical modulo one fingerprint at the parent — need to explain that one fingerprint!). \n\nWait — the fingerprint difference at parent 8f50fda: 087c951d7644