h under 4GB but previously crossed the stop — those scans now complete. Ranks whose targets partition already worked. Ranks that relied on bounded -stats/graph-json of fitting targets. Findings themselves: store swap is behaviour-neutral for findings (same graph content; BadgerGraph vs IntStore both implement usg.Store); fingerprints derive from rule id + location + concept, not store internals — so no rank's findings move.\n\nHmm, do I know that no finding moved for the driver subtree engine A/B? Summary says \"driver subtree alone → 105 findings, matching the rank's recorded base number (engine change preserves it)\". Good.\n\n- Also the adjacent pre-existing issue to name: a pathological target whose per-partition lowering density exceeds the ceiling (12 files × 2000 same-named functions) still hits the stop inside a partition — separate graph-per-byte density problem, verified identical at base, out of scope.\n\n**considered_and_rejected**:\n1. Raising memoryStopThreshold or the budget shares — treats the symptom; the disk store's cost is structural (structural core never leaves RAM in either store), and loosening the mandated 4GB cap is not the engine's to do.\n2. Always UseIntStore for bounded scans (drop DiskStorePath arming except when needed) — the disk store is still the bound for needsGraph runs that must serialise one graph too big for the ceiling (-stats, graph-json with review flags, where partitioning is disabled); removing the arming would regress those.\n3. Deciding the store inside extract.BuildGraph / lowering (newGraphStore) — would move cmd-level ceiling policy (scanSourceLimit/scanSourceBudget are cmd/vyql's) into internal/, and the same call serves the partitioned path which already forces IntStore; keeping the decision with the other ceiling decisions in cmd/vyql.\n4. Shrinking BadgerGraph's buffers instead — the 512MB detail buffer and 256MB cache at 4GB don't explain 4626−1220 = 3406MiB; the cost is the write path (memtables, SST builders, encode buffers) plus resident core, so tuning shares cannot close a 3.8× gap.\n5. Fixing the partition-density blowup (lowering cost per byte for pathological same-named functions) — real but separate gap; named as residual.\n6. Making the whole-repo scan partition openrazer — it fits one graph (PlanPartitions returns nil at 4GB); partitioning a fitting target would lose cross-package flows needlessly.\n\n**reviewer_should_know**:\n- What is closed: whole-repo mandated scan of rank 2794's clone now completes under 4GB/180s with the full 208 findings; repository-level proof is producible.\n- The one-fingerprint difference between the clone's two revisions is source-level (the fix commit adds the clamp), not engine-level; engine neutrality evidenced by the driver subtree's 105 matching the rank's recorded base and by the unbounded path being untouched.\n- Rank 2794 itself was ATTENTION for definition-side reasons (its file-scoped binding's cross-translation-unit residual); this closes the engine wall it named, it does not flip the rank by itself.\n- Residual: partition-internal lowering density blowup on pathological targets (pre-existing, identical at base).\n- The store swap changes nothing about findings: both stores implement usg.Store, fingerprints come from rule id + location + concept.\n- Peak-RSS numbers were sampled from /proc/<pid>/status VmRSS (no /usr/bin/time on this machine).\n- harness.err/harness.log are the harness's own snapshot files, left untracked.\n- Benchmarks: no OWASP corpus on this machine.\n\nLet me also double check the claim \"208 findings exit 3\" — exit 3 = check ran and did not pass (findings at/above fail-on). Fine.\n\nOne more check before writing: the fingerprint that differs — I should state what it is carefully: at fix 33aa7f07 the whole-clone scan reports 208 findings; at parent 8f50fda it reports 208 findings; comparing the two reports, exactly one finding's fingerprint differs between them. This is between source revisions, not between engines.\n\nAlso confirm timing claim \"~8s\" for both the failing base run (died at ~8s) and the fixed run (completed in 8s). From summary: base dies at 3.3GiB in ~8s exit 1; fixed: 208 findings, 8s, peak 1341MiB. Good — nice symmetry to state: same 8 seconds, one dies and one finishes.\n\nAlso worth stating in verification: the threshold is 3328MiB (3.3GiB) — displays as 3.3 GiB; peak after fix 1341MiB → margin ~1.99GB... Actually 3328−1341 = 1987MiB ≈ 1.9GB margin. The changelog says \"2.5GB margin\" — hmm: 4626 vs 1220 was the store comparison. The margin claim in changelog: \"the mandated 4GB ceiling stopped one and carries the other at a 2.5GB margin\". 4096MiB (4GB = 4e9 bytes ≈ 3906MiB; but the stop threshold is 3.5GB×... let me not over-derive: memoryStopThreshold(4GB) = 3.5GB? The summary says `memoryStopThreshold(4GB)` = 3.5GB (displays \"3.3 GiB\"). So 4GB decimal = 4e9 bytes = 3814.7 MiB; 3.5GB decimal = 3356 MiB ≈ displays 3.3GiB (3356/1024 = 3.28 GiB). And the peak 1341MiB → margin vs threshold 3356−1341 = 2015MiB ≈ 2GB decimal. Changelog says 2.5GB margin — hmm, 3328MiB threshold? 3328−1341=1987MiB. Either way ~2GB decimal. The changelog says \"carries the other at a 2.5GB margin\" — that's slightly off from ~2GB. Let me reconsider: maybe margin was computed vs the 1220MiB IntStore peak measured in the isolation experiment: 3356−1220 = 2136MiB ≈ 2.1GB. Or the whole-clone peak 1341: 3328−1341 = 1987MiB = 1.94GiB ≈ 2.0GB decimal. So \"2.5GB\" overstates. I should fix the changelog to say \"a 2GB margin\" or \"roughly 2GB\". Precision matters in this repo's culture. Let me correct the CHANGELOG line before... wait, I already committed. I can amend the commit — nothing has been pushed, amending is safe within my worktree. The campaign says work counts only committed; amending before writing the verdict is fine.\n\nActually, let me recompute honestly:\n- 4GB (the flag) = 4,000,000,000 bytes = 3814.7 MiB = 3.72 GiB.\n- memoryStopThreshold = 7/8 of it? The summary says memoryStopThreshold(4GB) = 3.5GB (i.e., 7/8 × 4GB = 3.5e9 bytes = 3340 MiB = 3.26 GiB, displays \"3.3 GiB\"). And verification noted \"threshold 3328MiB\" — 3328 MiB = 3.25 GiB. Hmm 3328MiB = 3489665024 bytes ≈ 3.49e9 ≈ 3.5GB decimal. Yes: 3.5GB decimal = 3340 MiB; 3328MiB is 3.4866e9. Close enough — the threshold displays 3.3 GiB (3328/1024 = 3.25 GiB). OK so threshold ≈ 3328 MiB.\n- Fixed whole-clone peak: 1341 MiB. Margin = 3328 − 1341 = 1987 MiB ≈ 1.94 GiB ≈ 2.08 GB decimal.\n- In-RAM store peak in the isolation experiment: 1220 MiB. Margin = 2108 MiB ≈ 2.06 GiB ≈ 2.21 GB decimal.\n\nSo \"a 2.5GB margin\" is wrong; ~2GB is right. Fix the changelog: change \"carries the other at a 2.5GB margin\" → \"carries the other with 2GB to spare\". Also the changelog says \"so the mandated 4GB ceiling stopped one and carries the other at a 2.5GB margin\" — reword: \"so the mandated 4GB ceiling stopped one and carries the other with roughly 2GB to spare\".\n\nAnd in the commit message — the commit message doesn't state the margin, so only the CHANGELOG needs the amend.\n\nLet me amend: edit CHANGELOG, `git add CHANGELOG.md`, `git commit --amend --no-edit`. Then get the new full SHA and write the verdict.","signature":"ac25dd8fcbc6496c9697a23b"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"849a66be-d02b-4472-8ef5-6034c5079727","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:11:34.641Z"}
