{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202609170711379a99dc228b0c45ee","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_82435ef7defd4f9d85002c38","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/campaign/work/engine-the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan.answer.json","content":"{\n  \"gap\": \"the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan\",\n  \"status\": \"DONE\",\n  \"commit\": \"31d5f496e3978c99ed91893c6cc388735759fe90\",\n  \"commits\": [\n    \"31d5f496e3978c99ed91893c6cc388735759fe90\"\n  ],\n  \"branch\": \"engine-work/the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan\",\n  \"base\": \"522a598a1dc547c02c2173af9b536d000dbe06bd\",\n  \"changed_files\": [\n    \"cmd/vyql/main.go\",\n    \"cmd/vyql/maxram_test.go\",\n    \"CHANGELOG.md\"\n  ],\n  \"blocked_ranks\": [\n    2794\n  ],\n  \"engine_gap\": null,\n  \"what_was_missing\": \"applyMaxRAM (cmd/vyql/main.go) armed the disk-backed BadgerGraph store for EVERY scan it bounded, and the one-graph scan path never revisited that choice. A bounded scan of a target that fits one graph under its ceiling then paid the store's cost on top of the graph it spared: BadgerGraph keeps the structural core (ids, types, adjacency, labels) resident in RAM exactly as IntStore does, and adds a detail buffer, a badger write path (memtable arenas, SST builders, encode buffers) and block caches beside it — so for a target the ceiling comfortably holds, the bounded route was several times MORE expensive than the unbounded store it was protecting.\\n\\nThe two other bounded routes already knew this: scanPartitionsCollect forces lowering.UseIntStore=true around its whole loop ('a partition is sized to hold a graph the ceiling can afford in RAM'), and the unwritable-cache-directory fallback sets UseIntStore=true outright. Only the plain one-graph bounded scan — precisely the scan the partition planner itself deems affordable, because extract.PlanPartitions returns nil for it — was routed through the disk store.\\n\\nMeasured on rank 2794's openrazer clone (the gap's named target): the whole-repository campaign-scan (timeout 180, --max-ram 4GB, --cache off) died at the 3.3GiB memory safety stop in ~8s, exit 1, empty report, on every attempt and with either revision's definitions — the wall is definition-independent, exactly as the rank's verdict recorded ('campaign-scan over repo/ under the mandated limits stops at the engine's memory safety threshold (3.3 GiB of its 4GB ceiling) with an empty report on every attempt'). The trigger is the Python graph over pylib + daemon + scripts (90 files): each subtree alone scans in seconds; only the combination explodes. Isolating the cause by measurement: lowering is NOT it (finishes in ~2s with node counts additive across subtrees); the disk store is — peak resident memory sampled from /proc/<pid>/status VmRSS was 4626MiB through BadgerGraph against 1220-1259MiB through IntStore on the same target, a 3.8x difference, and the stop fired during the bindings phase just before the scan would have finished. Forcing the in-RAM fallback (pointing HOME/XDG_CACHE_HOME/TMPDIR at unwritable directories so the store cannot be created) made the SAME 4GB scan complete — the decisive experiment. So under the mandated cap no rule's repository-level proof could be produced, and rank 2794 had to prove from the driver subtree alone.\",\n  \"what_changed\": \"cmd/vyql/main.go: one new 10-line helper, holdOneGraphInRAM(paths, excludes), plus two lines of wiring in scanPathsWithProfileDemand — the smallest change that makes the store choice expressible, and nothing else.\\n\\nThe helper decides the store by the SAME affordability measure that already decides partitioning: when a disk store is armed (lowering.DiskStorePath != \\\"\\\"), a source limit is in force (scanSourceLimit > 0), and extract.PlanPartitions(paths, excludes, scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget) returns nil — the partition planner's own statement that one graph holds this target — it sets lowering.UseIntStore=true around extract.BuildGraph and restores the previous value after, returning the restore func. In every other case it returns a no-op: unbounded scans (DiskStorePath == \\\"\\\"), and runs that must serialise one graph too big for the ceiling (-stats, graph-json with review flags), which keep the disk-backed store as their bound. A target OVER the one-graph limit is not routed by the helper at all — the plain scan path partitions it (and each partition already builds IntStore), and the needsGraph paths keep the spill.\\n\\napplyMaxRAM's doc comment now points at holdOneGraphInRAM for the one-graph case, mirroring the pointer it already had to scanPartitionsCollect for the partitioned case. No store implementation changed, no internal/ package changed, no vyql/ definition changed; IntStore and BadgerGraph both implement usg.Store, so the graph content, findings and fingerprints are store-independent.\",\n  \"test\": \"cmd/vyql/maxram_test.go, in the package that changed.\\n\\nTestBoundedOneGraphScanHoldsItsGraphInRAM: arms applyMaxRAM(\\\"8GB\\\") (with cacheHome(t) and memory-limit save/restore, the file's established idiom), scans partitionFixture(t, 3) through scanPathsWithProfileDemand, and asserts the returned store is not *usg.BadgerGraph. Verified RED at base 522a598a1d — with main.go restored to base and the test file left in place it fails with 'a target inside the one-graph limit was scanned on the disk-backed store' — and GREEN at HEAD.\\n\\nTestBoundedScanSpillsAGraphTheCeilingCannotHold: squeezes scanSourceLimit/scanSourceBudget to 1 so every target is over the limit, and asserts the store IS *usg.BadgerGraph — guarding the disk store's reason to exist. Passes at base and at HEAD, so the change cannot silently regress the spill path.\\n\\nSuites: CGO_ENABLED=1 VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/defs/vyql go test -count=1 ./... exits 0 over all 30 packages, including TestVyqlSpecs against the shipped definitions and the full cmd/vyql suite; gofmt clean; golangci-lint v2.12.1 reports 0 issues. (With VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/defs the outer checkout's duplicate vyql/ subdir fails two pre-existing tests identically at base and HEAD — an environment artifact, not this change.)\",\n  \"verification\": \"All scans below are the mandated campaign-scan (timeout 180, --max-ram 4GB, --cache off) over the openrazer clone at /campaign/work/rank-2794-1/repo, used read-only.\\n\\nAt base 522a598a1d: campaign-scan on the whole clone dies at the memory safety stop (3.3GiB of the 4GB ceiling) in ~8s, exit 1, empty report — reproduced with both the fix revision's and the batch base's definitions, confirming the wall is definition-independent.\\n\\nAt HEAD 31d5f496e3: the same command on the same clone completes — exit 3 (check ran and did not pass), 208 findings, ~8s wall (the same eight seconds in which the base run died), peak resident 1341MiB against the 3328MiB stop, roughly 2GB to spare. On the clone at parent 8f50fda: 208 findings, and comparing the two reports exactly one finding's fingerprint differs (087c951d764434db at the parent vs 2aa47a94d641bcfa at the fix) — a source-level difference between the two revisions (the fix commit adds the clamp), not an engine difference. On the driver subtree alone: 105 findings at both revisions, matching the 105 the rank's verdict recorded with the batch base's definitions — the engine change preserves that number. The repository-level proof rank 2794 could not produce is now producible: a whole-clone scan under the mandated cap with a full report.\\n\\nRoot-cause evidence, measured before writing any fix: peak RSS through the disk store 4626MiB vs 1220-1259MiB through IntStore on the same target (sampled from /proc/<pid>/status VmRSS; /usr/bin/time is not on this machine); lowering itself ~2s with additive node counts; and the same 4GB scan completing once the store directories were made unwritable and the pre-existing in-RAM fallback engaged. That fallback experiment is what the fix now reaches deliberately, by affordability, instead of by failure.\\n\\nBounded -stats and graph-json runs of fitting targets — which need the one graph and previously crossed the stop through the same store — now complete too, since the helper applies to every one-graph BuildGraph under a ceiling.\\n\\nFull go test -count=1 ./... green as under 'test'. Benchmarks: no OWASP or port corpus is present in this environment, so the protected benchmark jobs were not runnable here — said plainly, not skipped quietly. The unbounded code path (which those benchmarks run) is byte-for-byte the same route as before: holdOneGraphInRAM's first branch returns before doing anything when DiskStorePath is empty.\",\n  \"blast_radius\": \"Shared engine change; every rank's mandated scan goes through this code.\\n\\nWho changes behaviour: every bounded (-max-ram) scan that builds ONE graph for a target inside the one-graph limit now builds it on IntStore instead of BadgerGraph. Findings, counts and fingerprints do not move with the store — both stores implement usg.Store and fingerprints derive from rule id + location + concept, not store internals — verified directly: the driver subtree reports the same 105 findings the rank recorded under the base engine, and the whole clone's 208 findings are stable across the store swap. What moves is memory: such scans now peak at the graph's real footprint instead of several times it, so scans that crossed the safety stop now complete, and scans that were merely over-provisioned get cheaper. Any rank whose mandated campaign-scan target is a multi-package repository that fits one graph under 4GB but previously crossed the stop is unblocked by this — rank 2794 is the named one and is verified end to end.\\n\\nWho does not change: unbounded scans (no -max-ram — the OWASP benchmark route) take the DiskStorePath == \\\"\\\" early return and are code-path identical; partitioned scans already forced IntStore per partition; runs that must serialise one graph too big for the ceiling (-stats, graph-json with review flags on an over-limit target) keep the disk-backed store, guarded by TestBoundedScanSpillsAGraphTheCeilingCannotHold; the unwritable-cache fallback is untouched.\\n\\nThe campaign should re-run the span's benchmark jobs on hardware that has the corpora: this change makes bounded corpus runs cheaper, and if any harness runs its corpora under a -max-ram cap those runs will now take the IntStore route.\",\n  \"considered_and_rejected\": [\n    \"Raising the safety-stop threshold or re-partitioning applyMaxRAM's budget shares. Treats the symptom: the disk store's cost is structural (the structural core never leaves RAM in either store; the write path and caches sit beside it), and 4626MiB minus 1220MiB is ~3.4GB of overhead that no re-sharing of a 4GB budget absorbs. The mandated 4GB cap is also the campaign's constraint, not the engine's to relax.\",\n    \"Arming UseIntStore=true for every bounded scan and keeping DiskStorePath only for spills — the mirror of this fix, done inside applyMaxRAM. Rejected because applyMaxRAM arms once per process and cannot see the target: the store choice depends on whether THIS scan's target fits one graph and on whether the run must serialise one graph (needsGraph), both of which are known only at scan time in run()/scanPathsWithProfileDemand. Deciding there is also what scanPartitionsCollect already does for its own case.\",\n    \"Moving the decision into internal/extract/lowering (newGraphStore) so every caller benefits. Rejected: the affordability inputs (scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget) are cmd/vyql's ceiling policy, and newGraphStore also serves the partitioned path that already forces IntStore — pushing cmd-level policy into internal/ would be a wider change for the same behaviour.\",\n    \"Shrinking BadgerGraph's detail buffer and block cache instead of bypassing the store. The 512MB buffer and 256MB cache at a 4GB ceiling do not explain a 3.4GB delta; the remainder is the write path (memtable arenas, SST builders, encode buffers) and the resident core, so tuning shares cannot close a 3.8x gap.\",\n    \"Forcing the whole-clone scan to partition (lowering the one-graph limit) so each partition fits. openrazer fits one graph under 4GB — the planner says so — and partitioning a fitting target needlessly loses cross-package flows (a flow whose source and sink land in different partitions is not reported), which is exactly the fidelity a repository-level proof needs.\",\n    \"Also fixing the adjacent, pre-existing partition-density blowup: a pathological synthetic target (12 files x 2000 same-named Python functions, 1.9MB) plans into 3 partitions under 4GB yet a partition's lowering alone still crosses the stop — lowering cost per byte for generated-density code is a separate gap in the lowering, verified to behave identically at base. Named here as a residual, not smuggled into this change.\"\n  ],\n  \"reviewer_should_know\": \"What is closed: under the mandated 4GB cap and 180s timeout, a whole-repository scan of a multi-package target that fits one graph now completes and writes a full report — 208 findings in 8s at a 1341MiB peak on the gap's own target — so the repository-level proof rank 2794's verdict recorded as impossible ('campaign-scan over repo/ ... stops at the engine's memory safety threshold ... with an empty report on every attempt') is now producible with the same command.\\n\\nWhat this does NOT do for rank 2794, stated plainly: it closes the engine wall that rank named; it does not flip the rank by itself. The rank's own residual (its file-scoped C binding cannot see the fix's clamp in the other translation unit, so the finding fires at both revisions) is a definition-side property of that rank's lane, untouched here.\\n\\nResiduals and evidence caveats:\\n- The one fingerprint that differs between the clone's two revisions (087c951d764434db -> 2aa47a94d641bcfa) is a source-level difference — the fix commit adds the clamp — not an engine difference; engine neutrality is evidenced by the driver subtree's stable 105 findings and by the unbounded route being untouched.\\n- Peak-RSS figures were sampled from /proc/<pid>/status VmRSS in a loop (/usr/bin/time is not on this machine); the stop threshold displays as 3.3GiB (3328MiB at a 4GB flag).\\n- The partition-density blowup on pathological generated targets (previous item) remains: a target whose per-partition lowering alone exceeds the ceiling still dies at the stop, as at base. It needs a graph-per-byte measure in the lowering, which is a separate gap to file.\\n- No OWASP or port corpus exists in this environment, so the benchmark jobs were not runnable; the change's only route into them is bounded runs getting cheaper, and the unbounded route is unchanged.\\n- harness.err and harness.log are the harness's own snapshot files, present at session start and left untracked. The openrazer clone at /campaign/work/rank-2794-1/repo was used read-only and left at the fix commit 33aa7f07 as that rank's verdict recorded; the temporary git worktree this session briefly added under it was removed.\"\n}\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"6fac804b-4b6d-4edb-a460-169664a92ccb","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:12:26.920Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_82435ef7defd4f9d85002c38","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /campaign/work/engine-the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan.answer.json (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b7e732-1060-4c03-a560-f041798bec11","uuid":"4b9d9069-fb47-464a-bc67-adc2cb91b8af","timestamp":"2026-09-16T23:12:26.941Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/campaign/work/engine-the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan.answer.json","content":"{\n  \"gap\": \"the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan\",\n  \"status\": \"DONE\",\n  \"commit\": \"31d5f496e3978c99ed91893c6cc388735759fe90\",\n  \"commits\": [\n    \"31d5f496e3978c99ed91893c6cc388735759fe90\"\n  ],\n  \"branch\": \"engine-work/the-engine-s-memory-safety-stop-ends-a-whole-repository-scan\",\n  \"base\": \"522a598a1dc547c02c2173af9b536d000dbe06bd\",\n  \"changed_files\": [\n    \"cmd/vyql/main.go\",\n    \"cmd/vyql/maxram_test.go\",\n    \"CHANGELOG.md\"\n  ],\n  \"blocked_ranks\": [\n    2794\n  ],\n  \"engine_gap\": null,\n  \"what_was_missing\": \"applyMaxRAM (cmd/vyql/main.go) armed the disk-backed BadgerGraph store for EVERY scan it bounded, and the one-graph scan path never revisited that choice. A bounded scan of a target that fits one graph under its ceiling then paid the store's cost on top of the graph it spared: BadgerGraph keeps the structural core (ids, types, adjacency, labels) resident in RAM exactly as IntStore does, and adds a detail buffer, a badger write path (memtable arenas, SST builders, encode buffers) and block caches beside it — so for a target the ceiling comfortably holds, the bounded route was several times MORE expensive than the unbounded store it was protecting.\\n\\nThe two other bounded routes already knew this: scanPartitionsCollect forces lowering.UseIntStore=true around its whole loop ('a partition is sized to hold a graph the ceiling can afford in RAM'), and the unwritable-cache-directory fallback sets UseIntStore=true outright. Only the plain one-graph bounded scan — precisely the scan the partition planner itself deems affordable, because extract.PlanPartitions returns nil for it — was routed through the disk store.\\n\\nMeasured on rank 2794's openrazer clone (the gap's named target): the whole-repository campaign-scan (timeout 180, --max-ram 4GB, --cache off) died at the 3.3GiB memory safety stop in ~8s, exit 1, empty report, on every attempt and with either revision's definitions — the wall is definition-independent, exactly as the rank's verdict recorded ('campaign-scan over repo/ under the mandated limits stops at the engine's memory safety threshold (3.3 GiB of its 4GB ceiling) with an empty report on every attempt'). The trigger is the Python graph over pylib + daemon + scripts (90 files): each subtree alone scans in seconds; only the combination explodes. Isolating the cause by measurement: lowering is NOT it (finishes in ~2s with node counts additive across subtrees); the disk store is — peak resident memory sampled from /proc/<pid>/status VmRSS was 4626MiB through BadgerGraph against 1220-1259MiB through IntStore on the same target, a 3.8x difference, and the stop fired during the bindings phase just before the scan would have finished. Forcing the in-RAM fallback (pointing HOME/XDG_CACHE_HOME/TMPDIR at unwritable directories so the store cannot be created) made the SAME 4GB scan complete — the decisive experiment. So under the mandated cap no rule's repository-level proof could be produced, and rank 2794 had to prove from the driver subtree alone.\",\n  \"what_changed\": \"cmd/vyql/main.go: one new 10-line helper, holdOneGraphInRAM(paths, excludes), plus two lines of wiring in scanPathsWithProfileDemand — the smallest change that makes the store choice expressible, and nothing else.\\n\\nThe helper decides the store by the SAME affordability measure that already decides partitioning: when a disk store is armed (lowering.DiskStorePath != \\\"\\\"), a source limit is in force (scanSourceLimit > 0), and extract.PlanPartitions(paths, excludes, scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget) returns nil — the partition planner's own statement that one graph holds this target — it sets lowering.UseIntStore=true around extract.BuildGraph and restores the previous value after, returning the restore func. In every other case it returns a no-op: unbounded scans (DiskStorePath == \\\"\\\"), and runs that must serialise one graph too big for the ceiling (-stats, graph-json with review flags), which keep the disk-backed store as their bound. A target OVER the one-graph limit is not routed by the helper at all — the plain scan path partitions it (and each partition already builds IntStore), and the needsGraph paths keep the spill.\\n\\napplyMaxRAM's doc comment now points at holdOneGraphInRAM for the one-graph case, mirroring the pointer it already had to scanPartitionsCollect for the partitioned case. No store implementation changed, no internal/ package changed, no vyql/ definition changed; IntStore and BadgerGraph both implement usg.Store, so the graph content, findings and fingerprints are store-independent.\",\n  \"test\": \"cmd/vyql/maxram_test.go, in the package that changed.\\n\\nTestBoundedOneGraphScanHoldsItsGraphInRAM: arms applyMaxRAM(\\\"8GB\\\") (with cacheHome(t) and memory-limit save/restore, the file's established idiom), scans partitionFixture(t, 3) through scanPathsWithProfileDemand, and asserts the returned store is not *usg.BadgerGraph. Verified RED at base 522a598a1d — with main.go restored to base and the test file left in place it fails with 'a target inside the one-graph limit was scanned on the disk-backed store' — and GREEN at HEAD.\\n\\nTestBoundedScanSpillsAGraphTheCeilingCannotHold: squeezes scanSourceLimit/scanSourceBudget to 1 so every target is over the limit, and asserts the store IS *usg.BadgerGraph — guarding the disk store's reason to exist. Passes at base and at HEAD, so the change cannot silently regress the spill path.\\n\\nSuites: CGO_ENABLED=1 VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/defs/vyql go test -count=1 ./... exits 0 over all 30 packages, including TestVyqlSpecs against the shipped definitions and the full cmd/vyql suite; gofmt clean; golangci-lint v2.12.1 reports 0 issues. (With VYQL_HOME=/campaign/repos/defs the outer checkout's duplicate vyql/ subdir fails two pre-existing tests identically at base and HEAD — an environment artifact, not this change.)\",\n  \"verification\": \"All scans below are the mandated campaign-scan (timeout 180, --max-ram 4GB, --cache off) over the openrazer clone at /campaign/work/rank-2794-1/repo, used read-only.\\n\\nAt base 522a598a1d: campaign-scan on the whole clone dies at the memory safety stop (3.3GiB of the 4GB ceiling) in ~8s, exit 1, empty report — reproduced with both the fix revision's and the batch base's definitions, confirming the wall is definition-independent.\\n\\nAt HEAD 31d5f496e3: the same command on the same clone completes — exit 3 (check ran and did not pass), 208 findings, ~8s wall (the same eight seconds in which the base run died), peak resident 1341MiB against the 3328MiB stop, roughly 2GB to spare. On the clone at parent 8f50fda: 208 findings, and comparing the two reports exactly one finding's fingerprint differs (087c951d764434db at the parent vs 2aa47a94d641bcfa at the fix) — a source-level difference between the two revisions (the fix commit adds the clamp), not an engine difference. On the driver subtree alone: 105 findings at both revisions, matching the 105 the rank's verdict recorded with the batch base's definitions — the engine change preserves that number. The repository-level proof rank 2794 could not produce is now producible: a whole-clone scan under the mandated cap with a full report.\\n\\nRoot-cause evidence, measured before writing any fix: peak RSS through the disk store 4626MiB vs 1220-1259MiB through IntStore on the same target (sampled from /proc/<pid>/status VmRSS; /usr/bin/time is not on this machine); lowering itself ~2s with additive node counts; and the same 4GB scan completing once the store directories were made unwritable and the pre-existing in-RAM fallback engaged. That fallback experiment is what the fix now reaches deliberately, by affordability, instead of by failure.\\n\\nBounded -stats and graph-json runs of fitting targets — which need the one graph and previously crossed the stop through the same store — now complete too, since the helper applies to every one-graph BuildGraph under a ceiling.\\n\\nFull go test -count=1 ./... green as under 'test'. Benchmarks: no OWASP or port corpus is present in this environment, so the protected benchmark jobs were not runnable here — said plainly, not skipped quietly. The unbounded code path (which those benchmarks run) is byte-for-byte the same route as before: holdOneGraphInRAM's first branch returns before doing anything when DiskStorePath is empty.\",\n  \"blast_radius\": \"Shared engine change; every rank's mandated scan goes through this code.\\n\\nWho changes behaviour: every bounded (-max-ram) scan that builds ONE graph for a target inside the one-graph limit now builds it on IntStore instead of BadgerGraph. Findings, counts and fingerprints do not move with the store — both stores implement usg.Store and fingerprints derive from rule id + location + concept, not store internals — verified directly: the driver subtree reports the same 105 findings the rank recorded under the base engine, and the whole clone's 208 findings are stable across the store swap. What moves is memory: such scans now peak at the graph's real footprint instead of several times it, so scans that crossed the safety stop now complete, and scans that were merely over-provisioned get cheaper. Any rank whose mandated campaign-scan target is a multi-package repository that fits one graph under 4GB but previously crossed the stop is unblocked by this — rank 2794 is the named one and is verified end to end.\\n\\nWho does not change: unbounded scans (no -max-ram — the OWASP benchmark route) take the DiskStorePath == \\\"\\\" early return and are code-path identical; partitioned scans already forced IntStore per partition; runs that must serialise one graph too big for the ceiling (-stats, graph-json with review flags on an over-limit target) keep the disk-backed store, guarded by TestBoundedScanSpillsAGraphTheCeilingCannotHold; the unwritable-cache fallback is untouched.\\n\\nThe campaign should re-run the span's benchmark jobs on hardware that has the corpora: this change makes bounded corpus runs cheaper, and if any harness runs its corpora under a -max-ram cap those runs will now take the IntStore route.\",\n  \"considered_and_rejected\": [\n    \"Raising the safety-stop threshold or re-partitioning applyMaxRAM's budget shares. Treats the symptom: the disk store's cost is structural (the structural core never leaves RAM in either store; the write path and caches sit beside it), and 4626MiB minus 1220MiB is ~3.4GB of overhead that no re-sharing of a 4GB budget absorbs. The mandated 4GB cap is also the campaign's constraint, not the engine's to relax.\",\n    \"Arming UseIntStore=true for every bounded scan and keeping DiskStorePath only for spills — the mirror of this fix, done inside applyMaxRAM. Rejected because applyMaxRAM arms once per process and cannot see the target: the store choice depends on whether THIS scan's target fits one graph and on whether the run must serialise one graph (needsGraph), both of which are known only at scan time in run()/scanPathsWithProfileDemand. Deciding there is also what scanPartitionsCollect already does for its own case.\",\n    \"Moving the decision into internal/extract/lowering (newGraphStore) so every caller benefits. Rejected: the affordability inputs (scanSourceLimit, scanSourceBudget) are cmd/vyql's ceiling policy, and newGraphStore also serves the partitioned path that already forces IntStore — pushing cmd-level policy into internal/ would be a wider change for the same behaviour.\",\n    \"Shrinking BadgerGraph's detail buffer and block cache instead of bypassing the store. The 512MB buffer and 256MB cache at a 4GB ceiling do not explain a 3.4GB delta; the remainder is the write path (memtable arenas, SST builders, encode buffers) and the resident core, so tuning shares cannot close a 3.8x gap.\",\n    \"Forcing the whole-clone scan to partition (lowering the one-graph limit) so each partition fits. openrazer fits one graph under 4GB — the planner says so — and partitioning a fitting target needlessly loses cross-package flows (a flow whose source and sink land in different partitions is not reported), which is exactly the fidelity a repository-level proof needs.\",\n    \"Also fixing the adjacent, pre-existing partition-density blowup: a pathological synthetic target (12 files x 2000 same-named Python functions, 1.9MB) plans into 3 partitions under 4GB yet a partition's lowering alone still crosses the stop — lowering cost per byte for generated-density code is a separate gap in the lowering, verified to behave identically at base. Named here as a residual, not smuggled into this change.\"\n  ],\n  \"reviewer_should_know\": \"What is closed: under the mandated 4GB cap and 180s timeout, a whole-repository scan of a multi-package target that fits one graph now completes and writes a full report — 208 findings in 8s at a 1341MiB peak on the gap's own target — so the repository-level proof rank 2794's verdict recorded as impossible ('campaign-scan over repo/ ... stops at the engine's memory safety threshold ... with an empty report on every attempt') is now producible with the same command.\\n\\nWhat this does NOT do for rank 2794, stated plainly: it closes the engine wall that rank named; it does not flip the rank by itself. The rank's own residual (its file-scoped C binding cannot see the fix's clamp in the other translation unit, so the finding fires at both revisions) is a definition-side property of that rank's lane, untouched here.\\n\\nResiduals and evidence caveats:\\n- The one fingerprint that differs between the clone's two revisions (087c951d764434db -> 2aa47a94d641bcfa) is a source-level difference — the fix commit adds the clamp — not an engine difference; engine neutrality is evidenced by the driver subtree's stable 105 findings and by the unbounded route being untouched.\\n- Peak-RSS figures were sampled from /proc/<pid>/status VmRSS in a loop (/usr/bin/time is not on this machine); the stop threshold displays as 3.3GiB (3328MiB at a 4GB flag).\\n- The partition-density blowup on pathological generated targets (previous item) remains: a target whose per-partition lowering alone exceeds the ceiling still dies at the stop, as at base. It needs a graph-per-byte measure in the lowering, which is a separate gap to file.\\n- No OWASP or port corpus exists in this environment, so the benchmark jobs were not runnable; the change's only route into them is bounded runs getting cheaper, and the unbounded route is unchanged.\\n- harness.err and harness.log are the harness's own snapshot files, present at session start and left untracked. The openrazer clone at /campaign/work/rank-2794-1/repo was used read-only and left at the fix commit 33aa7f07 as that rank's verdict recorded; the temporary git worktree this session briefly added under it was removed.\"\n}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
